OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026", "title": "Generic Future Inextendibility with Square-Integrable Connection Near a Fixed Kerr Spacetime", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "264", "family_title": "Strong cosmic censorship near two-ended Kerr data", "abstract_html": "For each fixed rotating subextremal Kerr background with mass <i>M</i> &amp;gt; 0 and rotation \\(0\\lt |\\mathfrak a|\\lt M\\), we prove that the smooth vacuum data near its complete two-ended bridge whose full maximal globally hyperbolic development admits a future \\(C^0\\cap W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\) extension form a meagre set in the weighted smooth topology. The extension metric is continuous and nondegenerate, and its weak connection is locally square-integrable. The neighborhood requires smallness of only the tenth weighted seminorm, while the topology tests every finite order.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.2. The extension convention is Definition 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a Kerr vacuum spacetime with mass \\(M&gt;0\\) and signed rotation parameter \\(\\mathbf a\\) satisfying \\(0&lt;|\\mathbf a|&lt;M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d_*=(h_*,K_*)\\) be its smooth complete two-ended bridge data on \\(\\Sigma\\simeq\\mathbb R\\times S^2\\), with the inherited future orientation.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a smooth weight \\(\\rho\\ge1\\), equal to the radial coordinate outside a compact set on each end. Let \\(\\nabla_*\\) and \\(|\\cdot|_*\\) be the connection and tensor norms of \\(h_*\\). For smooth symmetric covariant two-tensors \\(u,v\\), define\n\\[\np_m(u,v)=\\sum_{j=0}^m\\left(\n\\sup_\\Sigma\\rho^{1+j}|\\nabla_*^ju|_*\n+\\sup_\\Sigma\\rho^{2+j}|\\nabla_*^jv|_*\n\\right),\\qquad m=0,1,\\ldots.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal D\\) consist of smooth pairs \\(d=(h,K)\\) for which \\(h\\) is positive definite and complete, every \\(p_m(d-d_*)\\) is finite, and\n\\[\nR(h)+(\\operatorname{tr}_hK)^2-|K|_h^2=0,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{div}_hK-d(\\operatorname{tr}_hK)=0.\n\\]\nGive \\(\\mathcal D\\) the relative topology generated by the seminorms \\(p_m\\), and put\n\\[\n\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon=\\{d\\in\\mathcal D:p_{10}(d-d_*)&lt;\\varepsilon\\}.\n\\]\nNo leading asymptotic coefficient, charge value, parity condition, or asymptotic expansion is imposed beyond these requirements.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(d\\in\\mathcal D\\), let \\((\\mathcal M_d,g_d)\\) be its full smooth maximal globally hyperbolic vacuum development.</p><p class=\"li\">A future \\(C^0\\cap W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\) extension consists of a connected time-oriented smooth four-manifold \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal M}\\), a continuous nondegenerate Lorentzian metric \\(\\widetilde g\\) of signature \\((-,+,+,+)\\) whose components belong to \\(W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\) in smooth charts, and a smooth time-orientation-preserving isometric embedding\n\\[\n\\iota:\\mathcal M_d\\hookrightarrow\\widetilde{\\mathcal M}\n\\]\nwith proper open image. There must be a future-directed timelike \\(C^1\\) curve \\(c:[0,1]\\to\\widetilde{\\mathcal M}\\) such that\n\\[\nc([0,1))\\subset\\iota(\\mathcal M_d),\n\\qquad\nc(1)\\in\\partial\\iota(\\mathcal M_d).\n\\]\nNo field equation or global-hyperbolicity assumption is imposed outside the image.</p><p class=\"li\">The connection condition is distributional. In a smooth chart,\n\\[\n\\Gamma^\\flat_{ijk}\n=\\frac12(\\partial_jg_{ik}+\\partial_kg_{ij}-\\partial_ig_{jk}).\n\\]\nThe identity\n\\[\n\\partial_kg_{ij}=\\Gamma^\\flat_{ikj}+\\Gamma^\\flat_{jki}\n\\]\nshows that \\(g\\in W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\) exactly when the lowered coefficients have locally \\(L^2\\) representatives. On compactly contained coordinate subdomains, continuity and nondegeneracy bound \\(g\\) and \\(g^{-1}\\), so raising or lowering the first index preserves \\(L^2\\). No trace on an individually prescribed curve is assumed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists \\(\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon(M,\\mathbf a)&gt;0\\) such that the data \\(d\\in\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\) whose full development admits a future extension of the specified type form a meagre subset of \\(\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\). Consequently, future \\(C^0\\cap W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\)-inextendible data contain a residual subset of \\(\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>Only \\(p_{10}\\) is required to be small. The topology tests every finite order. The neighborhood may depend on the fixed Kerr center; no assertion uniform toward zero rotation or extremality, no assertion outside these neighborhoods, and no openness assertion for inextendibility is made.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 1 reduces the fixed Kerr center to mass one and positive rotation, preserving every finite seminorm order and the extension class. Work in the single normalized \\(p_{10}\\) neighborhood fixed there.</p><p>By Proposition 5, all future-extendible data are covered by the countable family\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{O\\in\\mathcal B}\\bigcup_{B\\ge1}E(O,B).\n\\]\nBy Proposition 12, every member is relatively nowhere dense. Hence the extendible set is meagre.</p><p>Explicitly,\n\\[\n\\mathcal R\n=\\bigcap_{\\substack{O\\in\\mathcal B\\\\B\\ge1}}\n\\left(\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\setminus\\overline{E(O,B)}\\right)\n\\]\nis a countable intersection of relatively open dense sets and consists of future-inextendible data. The weighted constraint topology is Baire, as recorded in [22, Lemma 2.4].</p><p>The cover concerns every future exit of the full development. Background observations occur before first comparison runout; exact and persisted observations lie in finite interior regions of their full developments, where the full-lifetime witnesses apply. Thus no extension specified in Theorem 1 is omitted.</p><p>Finally pull back by the fixed normalization homeomorphism and restrict to an original \\(p_{10}\\) ball contained in the normalized neighborhood. Meagreness and the residual conclusion are preserved, proving the theorem for every fixed \\(M&gt;0\\), \\(0&lt;|\\mathbf a|&lt;M\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Section 2.1, Proposition 2.2, Convention 2.1, and equation (2.9)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the data space and extension convention of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">After normalization, fix the Kerr center \\(M=1\\), \\(0&lt;\\mathbf a&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The comparison-development inputs are the interfaces cited in the paper from [23, Propositions 6.4, 7.3–7.7, 8.2] and [22, Proposition 4.2].</p><p class=\"li\">A fixed finite-order bound \\(Q_T\\le e^{o(T)}\\) means that for every \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) there are \\(C_\\delta,T_\\delta\\) such that \\(Q_T\\le C_\\delta e^{\\delta T}\\) for \\(T\\ge T_\\delta\\). Constants may depend on the datum and the fixed orders. Uniformity for changing data is asserted only when separately proved.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The fixed-center problem reduces to \\(M=1\\), \\(0&lt;\\mathbf a&lt;1\\) by homeomorphisms preserving each finite seminorm order, the vacuum constraints, completeness, maximal developments, future orientation, and the extension class.</p><p>For one sufficiently small \\(p_{10}\\) neighborhood, every \\(d\\in\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\) has a smooth vacuum comparison development \\(\\mathcal P_d\\), with the entire bridge as Cauchy surface, embedded openly and isometrically in \\(\\mathcal M_d\\). It contains lower and exterior computing regions and a future double-null wedge attached at a spacelike cylinder \\(\\mathcal S\\simeq\\mathbb R\\times S^2\\). In the wedge,\n\\[\nt=\\frac{u+v}{2}\\ge0,\\qquad x=\\frac{v-u}{2},\\qquad \\mathcal S=\\{t=0\\},\n\\]\n\\[\ng=-2a\\,du\\,dv+\\gamma_{AB}(d\\theta^A-b^Adu)(d\\theta^B-b^Bdu),\n\\qquad\na=qA,\\qquad q=q_0e^{-\\kappa(u+v)},\n\\]\nwhere \\(q_0,\\kappa&gt;0\\). The letter \\(a\\) here is a metric coefficient and is unrelated to \\(\\mathbf a\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\ny_0=u,\\quad y_1=v,\\quad B_0=b,\\quad B_1=0,\n\\qquad\nL_i=\\partial_{y_i}+B_i,\\quad\n\\mathcal L_i=\\partial_{y_i}+\\mathcal L_{B_i},\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\n\\chi_i=\\frac12\\mathcal L_i\\gamma,\\quad\n\\ell_i=L_i\\log a,\\quad\n\\tau_i=\\operatorname{tr}_\\gamma\\chi_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\xi_0+\\xi_1=d_\\theta\\log A,\\qquad\n\\xi_0-\\xi_1=-a^{-1}\\gamma(\\partial_vb,\\cdot).\n\\]\nAngular Sobolev norms use a fixed sphere atlas, or equivalently a fixed reference connection.</p><p>The following estimates hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The fields \\(\\gamma^{\\pm1},A^{\\pm1}\\) are bounded in angular \\(H^6\\), and \\(\\xi_i,a^{-1}\\partial_vb\\) are bounded in angular \\(H^5\\). There are nonnegative measurable \\(g_i:\\mathbb R\\to[0,\\infty)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}\\sup_{[n,n+1]}g_i&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|\\chi_i\\|_{H^4}+\\|\\ell_i+\\kappa\\|_{H^4}+\\|B_i\\|_{H^5}\n\\le C(q+g_i(y_i)).\n\\]\nThe first pure longitudinal derivatives \\(\\mathcal L_i\\chi_i,L_i\\ell_i\\), and \\(\\partial_ub\\) in direction \\(0\\), have corresponding directional summable bounds through angular \\(H^2\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. As \\(t\\to\\infty\\) and \\(|u|,|v|\\to\\infty\\),\n\\[\n\\|b\\|_{H^5}+\\sum_i\\|(\\chi_i,\\ell_i+\\kappa)\\|_{H^4}\\to0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=0}^1\\left(\n\\|\\partial_{y_k}b\\|_{H^2}\n+\\sum_i\\|\\partial_{y_k}(\\chi_i,\\ell_i)\\|_{H^2}\n\\right)\\to0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=0}^1\\left(\n\\|\\partial_{y_k}\\gamma\\|_{H^4}\n+\\|\\partial_{y_k}\\log a+\\kappa\\|_{H^4}\n\\right)\\to0.\n\\]\nThese norms are bounded throughout the wedge.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For fixed \\(U\\in\\mathbb R\\), \\(C&gt;0\\), and each fixed ordinary derivative order,\n\\[\n\\gamma^{\\pm1},\\ \\log A,\\ b,\\ a^{-1}\\partial_vb\n\\]\nhave \\(e^{o(T)}\\) bounds on\n\\[\nu\\le U,\\qquad v\\le CT,\\qquad t\\ge0.\n\\]\nFixed derivatives of \\(a,\\sqrt a,\\partial_vb\\) retain the factors \\(a,\\sqrt a,a\\), respectively, with subexponential coefficients.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. In the normalized frame\n\\[\n\\widehat e_i=a^{-1/2}L_i,\\qquad \\widehat e_A=\\partial_A,\n\\]\nand directions \\(Z_i=L_i\\), \\(Z_A=\\sqrt a\\,\\partial_A\\), the connection matrices \\(V_\\alpha\\) are bounded and, in the bulk \\(\\min(t,v,-u)\\to\\infty\\),\n\\[\nV_0\\to D_0,\\qquad V_1\\to-D_0,\\qquad V_A\\to0,\n\\qquad\nD_0=\\operatorname{diag}(-\\kappa/2,\\kappa/2,0,0).\n\\]\nThe components of \\(a\\,\\mathrm{Riem}\\) in normalized slots and the differentiated connection arrays entering the wave operator are bounded and tend to zero there; fixed ordinary jets cost \\(e^{o(T)}\\).</p><p>Either branch may be ordered so that \\(v\\to+\\infty\\), by interchanging the eikonals and preparing the angular labels in the new shift-free direction.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(\\mathbf a&lt;0\\), the axial reflection \\(\\varphi\\mapsto-\\varphi\\), with Killing time unchanged, identifies the bridge and its future normal with those for \\(|\\mathbf a|\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\Psi:\\Sigma_{\\mathrm n}\\to\\Sigma\\) identify dimensionless normalized coordinates with the original bridge. Constant spacetime scaling by \\(M^{-2}\\) sends increments to\n\\[\n\\mathcal T(u,v)=(M^{-2}\\Psi^*u,M^{-1}\\Psi^*v).\n\\]\nThe normalized reference connection is the pullback of \\(\\nabla_*\\). Hence\n\\[\n|\\nabla_{\\mathrm n}^j(M^{-2}\\Psi^*u)|_{h_{*,\\mathrm n}}\n=M^j(|\\nabla_*^ju|_*)\\circ\\Psi,\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\nabla_{\\mathrm n}^j(M^{-1}\\Psi^*v)|_{h_{*,\\mathrm n}}\n=M^{j+1}(|\\nabla_*^jv|_*)\\circ\\Psi.\n\\]\nThe radial weights are comparable. For every finite \\(m\\),\n\\[\nC_m^{-1}p_m^{\\mathrm{orig}}(u,v)\n\\le p_m^{\\mathrm{norm}}(\\mathcal T(u,v))\n\\le C_mp_m^{\\mathrm{orig}}(u,v).\n\\]\nThe inverse image of a normalized \\(p_{10}\\) ball contains an original \\(p_{10}\\) ball. The transformations preserve the constraints, completeness, maximality, future orientation, and the extension convention. Smooth changes preserve local Sobolev regularity; constant scaling leaves the connection and timelike exits unchanged. Meagreness pulls back and restricts to the original open ball.</p><p>For the normalized center, choose a compact Kerr-parameter neighborhood away from zero rotation and extremality. The exact-profile construction in [23, Section 7.1] gives \\(r_-&gt;0\\), positive absolute inner surface gravity, and a common optical rate \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\). Choose \\(q_0\\) sufficiently small for both angular gauges and the exact-profile entry inequality, then choose the deeper computing exit, exterior constants, and data threshold.</p><p>The cited exterior closure, cylinder preparation, interior estimates, and joining construction give \\(\\mathcal P_d\\), its full-bridge Cauchy surface, and its embedding in \\(\\mathcal M_d\\). Their hypotheses require only the stated weighted bounds and \\(p_{10}\\) smallness. The cylinder is the wedge’s only relative boundary inside \\(\\mathcal P_d\\), so a future timelike track crossing it stays in the wedge until first runout.</p><p>The low characteristic estimates give the directional bounds and summable cell suprema. The pure-direction estimates supply the first longitudinal jets. Enlarge the envelopes to include these jets and replace their values on each unit interval by the interval supremum; the sum of closed-cell suprema increases by at most a factor two. Frozen-profile terms are \\(O(q)\\).</p><p>The fixed-order estimates of [23, Proposition 7.6] give the subexponential bounds. Differentiating \\(a=qA\\) and the relative-shift identity preserves the displayed lapse factors. The normalized connection and curvature assertions are [22, Lemma 4.5], equivalently [23, Lemma 9.3], using the low estimates and vacuum identities. No bound for curvature in an observer’s parallel frame is assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 2.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d\\in\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\) and use the comparison development of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">A seed \\(w\\in T\\Sigma\\) determines the future unit initial velocity\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1+h(w,w)}\\,n+w,\n\\]\nwhere \\(n\\) is the future unit normal.</p><p class=\"li\">All maximal geodesics are taken in the full \\(\\mathcal M_d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Identify \\(T\\Sigma\\) with \\(T^*\\Sigma\\) by \\(w\\mapsto h(w,\\cdot)\\), and let \\(\\mu_d\\) be the pullback of canonical symplectic volume.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every seed whose future geodesic has finite maximal proper lifetime in \\(\\mathcal M_d\\) crosses \\(\\mathcal S\\). Its first runout from \\(\\mathcal P_d\\) is either:</p><p class=\"li\">a branch, where one optical coordinate has a finite limit and the other tends to \\(+\\infty\\); or</p><p class=\"li\">a corner, where both optical coordinates tend to \\(+\\infty\\).</p><p>For each fixed datum, the seeds whose first runout is a corner form a \\(\\mu_d\\)-null set.</p>", "proof": "<p>The full-lifetime classification is [23, Corollary 8.6]. Corner removal is [22, Proposition 3.10]; its section-volume argument applies to every smooth positive measure on \\(T\\Sigma\\). Its geometric input is [23, Lemma 8.7], which assumes only the low interior bounds and a future unit timelike wedge track starting sufficiently far in both optical coordinates.</p><p>These statements classify first comparison runout. That event may precede the full geodesic endpoint in \\(\\mathcal M_d\\).</p><p>The section-volume statement used here and below follows from the unit geodesic Hamiltonian shell: the restriction of the symplectic form has the flow direction as kernel. A single-valued transverse hitting map therefore preserves the restricted two-form and its third exterior power, even when hitting time varies.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 2.4. Finite persistence is recorded in Section 2.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d\\in\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\) and a branch seed with ordering \\(u\\to u_*\\in\\mathbb R\\), \\(v\\to+\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On a transverse \\(v\\)-section use canonical state coordinates\n\\[\n(u,\\theta,\\pi_u,P),\n\\qquad\n\\pi_u=g(\\dot c,\\partial_u),\\quad\nP_A=g(\\dot c,\\partial_A).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A packet launch patch is a relatively compact coordinate patch in \\((v-T,\\theta)\\) on \\(\\mathcal S\\). It is admissible when the covectors \\(dv\\) have backward null rays crossing the entry, horizon, and original initial hypersurfaces transversely, escaping into the chosen exterior end, with the fixed-depth ray and collar bounds of [23, Propositions 9.6, 9.7].</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a relatively compact open seed neighborhood \\(N\\), as small as desired, such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. All seeds in \\(\\overline N\\) have compact earlier transverse flow tubes from \\(\\Sigma\\) to a fixed late \\(v\\)-section, and then remain in a common compact \\(u\\)-range and sphere chart. Their momenta are bounded and\n\\[\np=-(\\pi_u+b^AP_A)=a\\dot v\n\\]\nhas positive upper and lower bounds. Every such track has branch runout in the chosen ordering.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For a sufficiently small fixed interval \\(J\\) about zero and an unbounded sequence of \\(T\\), fixed-side-length product boxes \\(Q_T\\) in \\((u,v-T,\\theta)\\) contain all hits at \\(v\\in T+J\\). A fixed enlargement has common \\(u\\) and angular margins. The angular feet on \\(\\mathcal S\\) of all \\(L_0\\)-segments from that enlargement lie, with a fixed interior margin, in one admissible small launch patch. Its size is fixed independently of later derivative and accuracy choices.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For fixed \\(u_2&gt;u_*\\), \\(c_2&gt;0\\), and sufficiently small \\(c_0&gt;0\\), all entry-to-observation portions and enlarged observation boxes fit with strict outer margins in\n\\[\n0\\le t\\le L_T=\\frac{T+u_*}{2}+c_0,\n\\qquad\nu\\le u_2-\\frac{c_0t}{T},\n\\qquad\nv\\le T+c_2-\\frac{c_0t}{T}.\n\\]\nThe slice \\(x\\)-intervals have a positive lower length uniformly for large \\(T\\). No entry margin from \\(t=0\\) is required.</p><p>For every fixed finite observation, compact-subset smooth Cauchy stability preserves strict observation and transversality inequalities under sufficiently small changes of smooth constraint data. The neighborhood may depend on the observation time and finite derivative orders; no continuity of maximal geodesic lifetime is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\Xi=\\dot\\theta-b\\dot u\\), so \\(P_A=\\gamma_{AB}\\Xi^B\\). Unit normalization gives\n\\[\n2a\\dot u\\dot v=1+|\\Xi|_\\gamma^2,\\qquad p=-g(\\dot c,L_0)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe low connection identities imply\n\\[\n|\\dot{\\log p}|\\le C(\\dot u+|\\Xi|_\\gamma).\n\\]\nThe shape term is controlled by \\(|\\Xi|_\\gamma^2/p\\le2\\dot u\\), and the lapse and torsion coefficients are bounded. On a branch, \\(\\int du&lt;\\infty\\) and \\(\\int a\\,dv&lt;\\infty\\), so\n\\[\n\\int|\\Xi|_\\gamma\\,d\\tau\n\\le C\\left(\\int du\\int a\\,dv\\right)^{1/2}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThus \\(p,p^{-1}\\) are bounded, and the angular track has finite length and eventually stays in one chart.</p><p>The reduced \\(v\\)-Hamiltonian is\n\\[\nH_v=-\\pi_v=\\frac{a(1+\\gamma^{AB}P_AP_B)}{2p},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{du}{dv}=\\frac{a(1+\\gamma^{AB}P_AP_B)}{2p^2}.\n\\]\nIts angular equation gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{dP}{dv}\\right|\n\\le C(p+|P|)\\frac{du}{dv}.\n\\]\nGronwall in the bounded increasing variable \\(u\\) bounds \\(P\\), hence \\(\\Xi,\\dot u,\\pi_u\\).</p><p>Choose a compact state neighborhood late on the track, with strict margins and \\(p\\) separated from zero. Every fixed state derivative of the Hamiltonian is bounded by\n\\[\nCe^{-\\kappa v}e^{o(v)}.\n\\]\nThe field and its Lipschitz bound are integrable in \\(v\\). Starting sufficiently late and shrinking the state neighborhood keeps all nearby states inside the margins forever. Compact earlier flow transports this neighborhood to \\(N\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\Phi_v(u,\\theta)\\) be the angular foot at \\(u=-v\\) of the \\(L_0\\)-characteristic through \\((u,v,\\theta)\\). Its variational equation gives\n\\[\n\\|D_\\theta\\Phi_v\\|+\\|(D_\\theta\\Phi_v)^{-1}\\|\n\\le C\\exp\\left(C\\int_{-v}^{u}(q+g_0(s))\\,ds\\right)\\le C.\n\\]\nThe terminal \\(u\\)-derivative contributes bounded \\(b\\). The \\(v\\)-derivative contributes the moving endpoint and an integral of \\(\\partial_vb=O(a)\\); since \\(\\int_{-v}^{u}a\\,ds\\le C\\), these are uniformly bounded. The foot map is therefore uniformly Lipschitz.</p><p>Shrink \\(N,J\\). Compactness of the sphere gives an unbounded subsequence of central feet in one small chart patch with margins. The fixed-patch refinements [22, Section 4.5, Corollary 4.8] apply uniformly there; increasing later finite beam orders changes constants and accuracy, not the patch size. Fixed product-box radii then contain all hits and retain the launch-foot margin.</p><p>Choose \\(c_0\\) small relative to \\(u_2-u_*\\) and \\(c_2\\), and shrink the state and box widths. Future causal vectors have \\(du,dv\\ge0\\), and \\(t\\) is temporal. Thus the side functions \\(u+c_0t/T\\) and \\(v+c_0t/T\\) decrease toward the past. The slice interval length is\n\\[\nT+c_2+u_2-2t-\\frac{2c_0t}{T}.\n\\]\nAt \\(t=L_T\\) it tends to \\(c_2+u_2-u_*-3c_0&gt;0\\); earlier intervals are longer.</p><p>Finite persistence is the compact smooth Cauchy-stability statement [23, Lemma 2.2], also used in [22, Lemma 3.3], obtained from local wave reductions, domain of dependence, and geometric uniqueness. Each use concerns a fixed compact experiment.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 3.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this proposition only, replace the near-Kerr setting by a smooth time-oriented globally hyperbolic four-dimensional spacetime \\((\\mathcal M,g)\\) with smooth spacelike Cauchy surface \\(\\Sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Identify \\(\\mathcal M\\) with a proper open image in a smooth ambient manifold carrying a continuous nondegenerate \\(W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\) Lorentzian metric \\(\\widetilde g\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume a future timelike \\(C^1\\) curve exits \\(\\mathcal M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All geodesics and Hamiltonian flows are those of the smooth metric on the full \\(\\mathcal M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(T\\Sigma\\), use the section measure pulled back from canonical volume on \\(T^*\\Sigma\\) by \\(w\\mapsto h(w,\\cdot)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a measurable positive-section-measure family \\(S\\subset T\\Sigma\\) such that every future unit geodesic with seed in \\(S\\) has finite maximal future proper lifetime in the full \\(\\mathcal M\\), and an ambient endpoint on \\(\\partial\\mathcal M\\). The endpoints lie in one compact subset of an ambient chart.</p><p>There are finitely many precompact ambient charts\n\\[\n\\psi_j:U_j\\to V_j\\subset\\mathbb R^4\n\\]\nand constants \\(C&lt;\\infty\\), \\(m&gt;0\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li\">throughout every \\(V_j\\), the metric and inverse metric have component norms at most \\(C\\), and\n\\[\n\\|\\widetilde\\Gamma_j\\|_{L^2(V_j)}\\le C;\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">at every proper time from the intersection with \\(\\Sigma\\) to, but excluding, the full future endpoint, at least one chart satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(\\psi_j(\\gamma(\\tau)),\\mathbb R^4\\setminus V_j\\bigr)\\ge m,\n\\qquad\n|(\\psi_j)_*\\dot\\gamma(\\tau)|\\le C.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemmas 4.2–4.3. Choose small \\(\\rho_0,\\varepsilon_0&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\nL\\rho_0&lt;\\frac14,\\qquad\n2\\varepsilon_0+C_\\beta(\\rho_0+2\\varepsilon_0)&lt;c,\n\\qquad\n\\rho_0+4\\varepsilon_0&lt;1,\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_\\beta\\) bounds the derivative of a fixed bump on \\((-1,1)\\) equal to one at zero. Fix \\(K&gt;3\\). For a small box write\n\\[\ns_c=f(z_c),\\qquad s_0=s_c-l,\\qquad s_1=s_c+l.\n\\]\nAll starts \\((s_0,z)\\), \\(|z-z_c|&lt;\\rho_0l\\), lie below the graph and in \\(I^+(\\Sigma)\\).</p><p>Parameterize unit velocities by \\(|\\eta|&lt;\\varepsilon_0\\):\n\\[\nU=N(1,\\eta),\\qquad\nN=[-\\widetilde g((1,\\eta),(1,\\eta))]^{-1/2},\n\\qquad\n\\pi_i=N(\\widetilde g_{i0}+\\widetilde g_{ij}\\eta^j).\n\\]\nOn the wider cone, \\(N\\) has positive upper and lower bounds. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial\\pi_i}{\\partial\\eta^j}\n=N\\widetilde g_{ij}\n+N^3(\\widetilde g_{i0}+\\widetilde g_{ik}\\eta^k)\n(\\widetilde g_{j0}+\\widetilde g_{jk}\\eta^k).\n\\]\nThis Jacobian is uniformly positive definite and makes \\(\\eta\\mapsto\\pi\\) injective on the slope ball. The canonical initial state set \\(A_l\\) satisfies\n\\[\nc_1l^3\\le|A_l|\\le C_1l^3,\n\\]\nand all pre-stop momenta remain in a fixed bounded set.</p><p>Follow the smooth geodesic in the full \\(\\mathcal M\\), parameterized by \\(s\\), stopping at \\(s_1\\), a box face, or \\(|\\eta|=2\\varepsilon_0\\). Before these stops,\n\\[\n|z(s)-z_c|\\le(\\rho_0+4\\varepsilon_0)l&lt;l,\n\\]\nso no spatial face is reached. The pre-stop domain is open. Its \\(s\\)-section hitting maps are smooth, injective, and have canonical Jacobian one. Indeed, on\n\\[\nH=\\tfrac12g^{\\alpha\\beta}\\pi_\\alpha\\pi_\\beta=-\\tfrac12,\n\\qquad\n\\partial_{\\pi_0}H=\\dot s&gt;0,\n\\]\nsolve \\(\\pi_0=-h(s,z,\\pi)\\). The reduced equations are\n\\[\n\\frac{dz^i}{ds}=\\partial_{\\pi_i}h,\\qquad\n\\frac{d\\pi_i}{ds}=-\\partial_{z^i}h,\n\\]\nand have zero divergence in \\((z,\\pi)\\).</p><p>For\n\\[\nI(x)=\\int_{\\text{before stop}}|\\Gamma_{\\mathcal M}(\\gamma_x(s))|\\,ds,\n\\]\ncanonical volume preservation, Tonelli, the bounded momentum fibers, and Lemma 4.3 give\n\\[\n\\int_{A_l}I(x)\\,dx\n\\le C\\int_{Q_l(z_c)}|\\widetilde\\Gamma|\\,ds\\,dz=o(l^3).\n\\]\nThe weak and smooth arrays agree only almost everywhere, but the phase change of variables and Fubini show that their exceptional chart-null set has zero time measure on almost every arc. No Sobolev trace is prescribed.</p><p>The slope equation is\n\\[\n\\frac{d\\eta^i}{ds}\n=-\\Gamma^i_{\\alpha\\beta}(1,\\eta)^\\alpha(1,\\eta)^\\beta\n+\\eta^i\\Gamma^0_{\\alpha\\beta}(1,\\eta)^\\alpha(1,\\eta)^\\beta.\n\\]\nThus \\(|d\\eta/ds|\\le C_2|\\Gamma_{\\mathcal M}|\\). A set \\(G_l\\subset A_l\\) of measure at least \\(|A_l|/2\\) has\n\\[\nI(x)&lt;\\varepsilon_0/(2C_2).\n\\]\nIts slopes stay below \\(3\\varepsilon_0/2\\), so neither slope nor chart-face stop occurs.</p><p>An arc in \\(G_l\\) surviving through \\(s_1\\) would contradict Lemma 4.1. Namely, take a bump \\(\\beta\\) supported in \\((s_0,s_1)\\), with \\(\\beta(s_c)=1\\), \\(|\\beta'|\\le C_\\beta/l\\), and set\n\\[\n\\Delta z=z_c-z(s_c),\\qquad\nz_r(s)=z(s)+r\\beta(s)\\Delta z.\n\\]\nThe bound \\(|\\Delta z|\\le(\\rho_0+2\\varepsilon_0)l\\) and the chosen constants keep every spatial slope below \\(c\\). The paths have fixed interior endpoints, but the final one passes through \\((s_c,z_c)\\in\\partial\\mathcal M\\).</p><p>Consequently each good arc has maximal pre-stop height \\(s_*\\le s_1\\). Unit normalization gives\n\\[\n0&lt;c_3\\le ds/d\\tau\\le C_3.\n\\]\nIts remaining proper time is finite and its bounded slope gives an ambient position limit in a compact subrectangle. An interior limit would give bounded velocity, a velocity limit by the smooth equation, and continuation; this contradicts either maximality or the preceding surviving-arc exclusion. The limit is therefore on \\(\\partial\\mathcal M\\). Any continuation in the full \\(\\mathcal M\\) would have the same boundary value, so this is the full future lifetime.</p><p>Every maximal timelike geodesic meets \\(\\Sigma\\). To justify causal inextendibility, near a hypothetical interior endpoint use a smooth temporal chart with bounded causal slopes. Then\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{ds}\\log\\frac{ds}{d\\tau}\n=-\\Gamma^0_{\\alpha\\beta}(1,\\eta)^\\alpha(1,\\eta)^\\beta\n\\]\nis bounded, giving finite affine duration, bounded velocity, and continuation. The same applies to past endpoints. The Cauchy property therefore gives one past hit of \\(\\Sigma\\) for these starts.</p><p>Smooth dependence and transverse hitting give a countable cover of \\(A_l\\) by smaller precompact neighborhoods on which the backward hit is a diffeomorphism and the entire earlier flow tube has compact closure in the interior unit shell. One meets \\(G_l\\) in positive measure. Restrict there and transport to \\(\\Sigma\\); canonical section volume is preserved.</p><p>Cover the compact earlier flow tube by finitely many ambient charts, choosing smaller neighborhoods to obtain a common positive margin. Compactness in the unit shell bounds velocities. Add the exit chart, where the narrow cone and normalization give the corresponding bounds. Taking maxima and minima gives the constants asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the spacetime and ambient setting of Proposition 4. Let \\(p,q\\in\\mathcal M\\), and let\n\\[\nH:[0,1]\\times[0,1]\\to\\widetilde{\\mathcal M}\n\\]\nbe continuous. Suppose every \\(H(r,\\cdot)\\) is a locally Lipschitz future causal path from \\(p\\) to \\(q\\), with causal tangent almost everywhere, and\n\\[\nH(0,[0,1])\\subset\\mathcal M.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The entire image of \\(H\\) lies in \\(\\mathcal M\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\nA=\\{r:H(r,[0,1])\\subset\\mathcal M\\}.\n\\]\nCompactness of the parameter interval and openness of \\(\\mathcal M\\) make \\(A\\) relatively open. For \\(r\\in A\\), the path is intrinsic and lies in the single compact diamond\n\\[\nK=J^+_{\\mathcal M}(p)\\cap J^-_{\\mathcal M}(q).\n\\]\nIts ambient image is compact and hence closed. If \\(r_n\\in A\\), \\(r_n\\to r\\), continuity gives \\(H(r,t)\\in K\\) for every \\(t\\). Thus \\(A\\) is also closed and nonempty, proving the assertion. Global hyperbolicity is used only inside \\(\\mathcal M\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the hypotheses of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a precompact ambient chart with coordinates \\((s,z)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3\\), a constant \\(c&gt;0\\), a bottom height \\(-d_0&lt;0\\), a ball \\(D\\), and a Lipschitz function \\(f:D\\to\\mathbb R\\), with Lipschitz constant \\(L&lt;\\infty\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(s\\) is temporal, and every \\((1,\\eta)\\) with \\(|\\eta|&lt;c\\) is uniformly future timelike.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\((-d_0,z)\\in I^+_{\\mathcal M}(\\Sigma)\\) for every \\(z\\in D\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The segment \\(-d_0\\le s&lt;f(z)\\) lies in \\(\\mathcal M\\), while \\((f(z),z)\\in\\partial\\mathcal M\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. \\(f(0)=0\\), and for some \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;d_0/4\\),\n\\[\n-\\delta&lt;f(z)\\le\\delta.\n\\]\nThe containing rectangle has upper face above \\(2\\delta\\).</p><p>The metric, inverse metric, positive spatial block, and timelike normalization on closed narrower cones have uniform bounds and nondegeneracy on this rectangle.</p>", "proof": "<p>A late segment of the exiting curve lies in \\(I^+_{\\mathcal M}(\\Sigma)\\): extend it to the past by a past-inextendible timelike curve. The resulting inextendible curve meets the Cauchy surface, and its remaining future lies in that chronological future.</p><p>Choose a small chart about the exit \\(b\\), a point \\(q\\) slightly to its future on a uniformly timelike coordinate line, and late interior points \\(x_n\\to b\\). The straight segments \\([x_n,q]\\) are uniformly future timelike. If one first exits, use it. Otherwise they all lie in \\(\\mathcal M\\), including \\(q\\), and for a fixed earlier \\(p\\) the points \\(x_n\\) lie in the compact intrinsic diamond \\(J^+(p)\\cap J^-(q)\\), contradicting \\(b\\notin\\mathcal M\\).</p><p>Translate the selected first exit to \\((0,0)\\), make the straight segment vertical, and take the other coordinate directions in its spacelike orthogonal hyperplane. Shrinking gives the temporal coordinate and fixed timelike cone, together with all algebraic metric bounds.</p><p>Choose the central bottom \\((-d_0,0)\\) inside \\(I^+(\\Sigma)\\), and define\n\\[\nf(z)=\\inf\\{s\\in[-d_0,2\\delta]:(s,z)\\notin\\mathcal M\\},\n\\qquad \\inf\\varnothing=2\\delta.\n\\]\nCompact openness below the central exit makes \\(f(z)&gt;-\\delta\\) on a small ball of radius \\(R\\), and choose \\(R/\\delta&lt;c/4\\).</p><p>If \\(f(z)&gt;\\delta\\), the vertical segment from \\(p_z=(-d_0,z)\\) to \\(q_z=(\\delta,z)\\) is interior. The broken path\n\\[\np_z\\longrightarrow(0,0)\\longrightarrow q_z\n\\]\nhas slopes less than \\(c/2\\). Linear interpolation of its spatial graph with the vertical segment is a fixed-endpoint timelike homotopy. Lemma 4.1 forbids the member through the exit. Hence \\(f(z)\\le\\delta\\), the clipping is inactive, and \\((f(z),z)\\in\\partial\\mathcal M\\).</p><p>Shrink further so \\(2R/(d_0-\\delta)&lt;c/2\\), and fix \\(L&gt;4/c\\). If\n\\[\nf(z')&gt;f(z)+L|z-z'|,\n\\]\nchoose\n\\[\nf(z)+2|z-z'|/c&lt;b'&lt;f(z').\n\\]\nThe path from \\((-d_0,z')\\) through \\((f(z),z)\\) to \\((b',z')\\) has both slopes less than \\(c/2\\). Its endpoints and vertical reference are interior. Lemma 4.1 again gives a contradiction. Interchanging \\(z,z'\\) proves the Lipschitz bound. Strict cone margins permit smoothing the corners.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the first-exit graph from Lemma 4.2. Fix \\(K&gt;1\\) and \\(D'\\Subset D\\), and put\n\\[\nQ_l(z)=\\{(s,y):|y-z|&lt;Kl,\\ |s-f(z)|&lt;Kl\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(l_n\\downarrow0\\) and \\(z_n\\in D'\\) such that these boxes lie in one fixed precompact subchart and\n\\[\nl_n^{-3}\\int_{Q_{l_n}(z_n)}|\\widetilde\\Gamma|\\,ds\\,dy\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On the compact chart region, \\(L^2\\) implies \\(L^1\\). The union of the boxes with centers in \\(D'\\) lies in\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_l=\\{(s,y):y\\in D'',\\ |s-f(y)|\\le K(1+L)l\\},\n\\qquad D'\\Subset D''\\Subset D.\n\\]\nFor each \\((s,y)\\), the possible centers have volume at most \\(C_Kl^3\\). Tonelli gives\n\\[\n\\int_{D'}l^{-3}\\int_{Q_l(z)}|\\widetilde\\Gamma|\\,ds\\,dy\\,dz\n\\le C_K\\int_{\\mathcal L_l}|\\widetilde\\Gamma|.\n\\]\nThe graph has four-dimensional measure zero by Fubini. Absolute continuity of the \\(L^1\\) integral makes the right side tend to zero. Choose a center with no more than the average normalized mass.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 3.6. The tests are Definition 3.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Return to the near-Kerr neighborhood fixed in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a countable basis \\(\\mathcal B\\) of coordinate balls in \\(T\\Sigma\\), with compact closures in members of a countable atlas and rational centers and radii.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(O\\in\\mathcal B\\) and an integer \\(B\\ge1\\), define \\(E(O,B)\\subset\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\) as follows.</p><p>A datum belongs to \\(E(O,B)\\) if:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. It has an extension of the type in Theorem 1 and a measurable \\(S\\subset O\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\mu_d(S)&gt;.99\\,\\mu_d(O),\n\\]\nsuch that all its seeds have finite full future lifetime. At most \\(B\\) precompact ambient charts witness the chart condition of Proposition 4 along each entire geodesic from \\(\\Sigma\\) to its endpoint. On every whole chart, the metric norm, inverse-metric norm, and Christoffel \\(L^2\\) norm are at most \\(B\\). At every point some chart has coordinate velocity at most \\(B\\) and distance from its coordinate boundary at least \\(1/B\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There is one neighborhood \\(N\\) as in Proposition 3 with \\(\\overline O\\subset N\\), ordered so \\(v\\to+\\infty\\), and one choice of central track, compact state ranges, positive \\(p\\) bounds, earlier transverse flow tube, interval \\(J\\), observation boxes, admissible launch patch, unbounded sequence of \\(T\\), and constants \\(u_2,c_2,c_0\\) serving every seed in \\(\\overline N\\).</p><p>All witnesses are existential for the given datum and are not additional countable indices. No closedness of \\(E(O,B)\\) is assumed. When several charts work, the least chart index gives a measurable selector.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The extendible set is exactly\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{O\\in\\mathcal B}\\ \\bigcup_{B=1}^{\\infty}E(O,B).\n\\]\nThis includes extensions whose actual exit occurs after first runout from the comparison development.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix an extendible datum. Proposition 4 gives a positive-measure seed family \\(S_*\\) with finite full lifetimes and finite uniform chart witnesses. Proposition 2 classifies first comparison runout and removes the corner subset as null.</p><p>Choose a density point \\(w\\in S_*\\) outside that null subset. It is a branch seed. Proposition 3, with optical interchange if needed, gives an open neighborhood \\(N\\) with the required tube, footprint, and finite-domain properties, independently of the ambient witnesses.</p><p>Choose \\(O\\in\\mathcal B\\) containing \\(w\\), with \\(\\overline O\\subset N\\), such that\n\\[\n\\mu_d(S_*\\cap O)&gt;.99\\,\\mu_d(O).\n\\]\nThe rational-ball basis suffices: small balls about a density point can be enlarged by arbitrarily small relative amounts to rational balls, and the smooth positive density preserves the limiting relative-complement estimate.</p><p>Set \\(S=S_*\\cap O\\), and choose \\(B\\) to dominate the number of charts, their bounds, and the reciprocal common margin. Thus \\(d\\in E(O,B)\\).</p><p>The observations precede first comparison runout. The finite full lifetime and chart witnesses were obtained independently for the entire geodesic in \\(\\mathcal M_d\\), so comparison runout need not be the actual exit. The reverse inclusion is part of the test definition.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 4.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a smooth \\(d\\in\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\), one branch tube and all witnesses from Proposition 3, and finite \\(m\\ge10\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Keep its central limit \\(u_*\\), neighborhood \\(N\\), interval \\(J\\), boxes \\(Q_T\\), admissible launch patch, unbounded sequence of \\(T\\), and constants \\(u_2,c_2,c_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Work on small enlargements of\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_T=\n\\left\\{\n0\\le t\\le L_T=\\frac{T+u_*}{2}+c_0,\\quad\nu+\\frac{c_0t}{T}\\le u_2,\\quad\nv+\\frac{c_0t}{T}\\le T+c_2\n\\right\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\kappa/4,\\qquad A_T=e^{-\\alpha T},\\qquad \\lambda=e^{\\zeta T},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) is chosen after all finite base orders and rate tolerances.</p><p class=\"li\">The launch bump is at least one on a neighborhood of the complete foot set of the enlarged observation boxes.</p><p class=\"li\">For a double-null metric, define\n\\[\n\\beta_i=\\operatorname{div}_\\gamma\\chi_i-d_\\theta\\tau_i,\\quad\nc_i=\\operatorname{curl}_\\gamma\\xi_j,\\quad\nQ_i=(K_\\gamma,c_i),\\quad\nm_i=K_\\gamma-\\operatorname{div}_\\gamma\\xi_j,\\qquad j\\ne i.\n\\]\nThe null equations whose defects are tested are\n\\[\nL_i\\tau_i=\\ell_i\\tau_i-|\\chi_i|_\\gamma^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_j\\chi_i\n=a\\,\\operatorname{Sym}\\nabla\\xi_j-\\frac a2K_\\gamma\\gamma\n+a\\xi_j^{\\otimes2}\n-\\frac12(\\tau_i\\chi_j+\\tau_j\\chi_i)+\\chi_i\\chi_j+\\chi_j\\chi_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_i\\xi_j=\\beta_i+\\tau_i\\xi_i,\n\\qquad\nL_j\\ell_i=aK_\\gamma-\\chi_i\\cdot\\chi_j+\\tau_i\\tau_j\n+a(2\\xi_i\\cdot\\xi_j-|\\xi_i|^2),\n\\]\ntogether with\n\\[\n\\partial_v\\gamma=2\\chi_1,\\quad\n\\partial_v\\log A=\\ell_1+\\kappa,\\quad\n\\partial_vb=qA\\gamma^{-1}(\\xi_1-\\xi_0),\n\\]\n\\[\n(L_i+\\kappa)(\\tau_i/A)=-A^{-1}|\\chi_i|^2.\n\\]\nThe angular system is\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_j\\beta_i\n=\\frac a2(dK_\\gamma+Jdc_i)\n+a(K_\\gamma*\\xi+\\xi*\\nabla\\xi+\\xi^3)\n+\\chi_i*\\nabla\\chi_j+\\chi_j*\\nabla\\chi_i,\n\\]\n\\[\nL_iQ_i=(\\operatorname{div},\\operatorname{curl})\\beta_i+R_i,\n\\qquad L_im_i=T_i,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nR_i,T_i:\\quad\n\\chi_i*(K_\\gamma,\\nabla\\xi)+\\nabla\\chi_i*\\xi.\n\\]\nHere \\(Jdf=(-\\partial_2f,\\partial_1f)\\) in oriented orthonormal components; \\(*\\) denotes smooth contractions with precisely the displayed differentiated factors.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every finite jet budget \\(N_{\\mathrm{jet}}\\), positive loss tolerance \\(\\eta_{\\mathrm{out}}\\), and \\(0&lt;\\nu&lt;1\\), one can choose \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) and finite construction orders so that, for arbitrarily large admissible \\(T\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. There are exact smooth complete vacuum data \\(d_T\\in\\mathcal D\\) on the whole bridge with\n\\[\np_m(d_T-d)\\to0.\n\\]\nThey agree with \\(d\\) on the other end, the connecting region, and an expanding protected ball on the selected end; the permitted exterior correction tail is retained.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Their exact exterior evolution reaches the required part of \\(\\mathcal S\\), with overlapping collars and strict outflow margins. In the fixed background wave-map description its entry jets differ from \\(g+h^p\\) by \\(O(A_T^2e^{\\eta_{\\mathrm{out}}T})\\), where\n\\[\nh^p=A_T\\,\\operatorname{tr.rev.}_g\n\\operatorname{Re}\\left(e^{i\\lambda v}\\sum_{j=0}^{J_*}\\lambda^{-j}p_j\\right).\n\\]\nThe leading coefficient is real, trace-free, annihilates \\(L_0\\), and satisfies throughout \\(Q_T\\)\n\\[\n|(p_0)_{AB}|_\\gamma\\ge e^{-o(T)}.\n\\]\nAt fixed required base orders, normalized amplitude jets are \\(e^{o(T)}\\), and linearized Ricci and gauge errors have arbitrarily high inverse-frequency accuracy.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. There is an exactly double-null template \\(\\bar g\\), with exact geometric derived fields, whose relative-field differences from \\(g\\), including \\(q^{-1}\\partial_vb\\), are \\(O(A_Te^{\\eta_{\\mathrm{out}}T})\\) through order \\(N_{\\mathrm{jet}}\\). Its null and angular defects, and its full entry-jet discrepancy from the actual exterior data in the initialized double-null gauge, are \\(O(A_T^2e^{\\eta_{\\mathrm{out}}T})\\).</p><p>All entry discrepancies, template changes, and defects vanish for \\(v&lt;(1-\\nu)T\\). Finite initial and residual buffers may be included. Positive frequency powers at fixed base orders are independent of later accuracy orders. The finitely many high actual entry norms required for nonlinear comparison have uniform \\(e^{o(T)}\\) bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an internal loss smaller than both \\(\\eta_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) and \\(\\alpha/4\\). Lemmas 6.1–6.2 give the exact data, exterior evolution, entry accuracy, protected agreement, and whole-footprint leading amplitude. Lemma 6.3 gives the linear double-null field variations.</p><p>Define the template by exact assignments\n\\[\n\\bar\\gamma=\\gamma+k_\\gamma,\\qquad\n\\bar A=Ae^{k_H},\\qquad\n\\bar a=q\\bar A,\\qquad\n\\bar b=b+k_b,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\bar g=-2\\bar a\\,du\\,dv\n+\\bar\\gamma_{AB}(d\\theta^A-\\bar b^Adu)(d\\theta^B-\\bar b^Bdu).\n\\]\nFor large \\(T\\), positivity and the low nondegeneracy bounds hold. Define all barred derived fields geometrically, not by truncated expansions:\n\\[\n\\bar\\chi_i=\\tfrac12(\\partial_{y_i}+\\mathcal L_{\\bar B_i})\\bar\\gamma,\n\\qquad\n\\bar\\ell_i=(\\partial_{y_i}+\\bar B_i)\\log\\bar a,\n\\qquad\n\\bar\\tau_i=\\operatorname{tr}_{\\bar\\gamma}\\bar\\chi_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\bar\\beta_i=\\operatorname{div}_{\\bar\\gamma}\\bar\\chi_i-d_\\theta\\bar\\tau_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\bar\\xi_0=\\tfrac12(d_\\theta\\log\\bar A-\\bar A^{-1}\\bar\\gamma q^{-1}\\partial_v\\bar b),\n\\quad\n\\bar\\xi_1=\\tfrac12(d_\\theta\\log\\bar A+\\bar A^{-1}\\bar\\gamma q^{-1}\\partial_v\\bar b),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\bar c_i=\\operatorname{curl}_{\\bar\\gamma}\\bar\\xi_j,\\quad\n\\bar Q_i=(K_{\\bar\\gamma},\\bar c_i),\\quad\n\\bar m_i=K_{\\bar\\gamma}-\\operatorname{div}_{\\bar\\gamma}\\bar\\xi_j.\n\\]</p><p>The null and angular equations are smooth finite-jet expressions in\n\\[\n\\gamma,\\gamma^{-1},A,A^{-1},b,H=\\log A,V=q^{-1}\\partial_vb\n\\]\nwith only nonnegative radial powers of \\(q\\). Cross-shape and lapse sources have factors \\(qA\\), shape products, and bounded relative fields; the angular principal coefficient is \\(qA/2\\), and its remaining terms have the listed \\(qA\\), shape, and angular-derivative factors. The normalized trace equation has no lapse derivative. Ordinary differentiation preserves the factor \\(q\\).</p><p>Substitution has zero background term and an arbitrarily accurate linearized vacuum term. Every remaining term has at least two increments. Through any fixed required order, its size is\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(A_T\\lambda^{-M}e^{C_{N_{\\mathrm{jet}}}T+o(T)}\n+A_T^2\\lambda^{P_{N_{\\mathrm{jet}}}}e^{o(T)}\\right).\n\\]\nInverse-\\(q\\) losses occur only on the linear residual, not on quadratic terms. Choose \\(\\zeta P_{N_{\\mathrm{jet}}}\\) below the allocated loss, then \\(M\\) large. This gives the quadratic defects and the linear template-change bound.</p><p>At entry \\(q=q_0\\). Eikonal roots, angular transport, and coordinate transformations have spacetime jets that are smooth finite-jet functions of the exterior metric and the prescribed coordinate values. Their linearization is precisely Lemma 6.3. Taylor expansion and the exterior entry comparison therefore give the quadratic actual-minus-template discrepancy. The gauges are compared only at fixed depth, not deep in the wedge.</p><p>First arrival, causal monotonicity, and optical uniqueness give exact vanishing in \\(v&lt;(1-\\nu)T\\).</p><p>The finite choice order is: choose output and bootstrap orders; include the finitely many higher initial and residual orders requested by nonlinear interpolation and continuation; choose rate and support tolerances; choose \\(\\zeta\\) for all positive frequency powers at these base orders; only then enlarge hierarchy, beam, Taylor, and matching accuracy orders. The latter do not increase previously budgeted positive powers. At the required high entry orders the changes are exponentially small and background jets are \\(e^{o(T)}\\). Finally take \\(T\\) large.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Section 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the fixed background, branch tube, launch footprint, amplitude, and frequency of Proposition 6. Let \\(P\\) be the background reduced linearized vacuum operator on trace-reversed two-tensors, \\(C_g=\\operatorname{div}_g\\), and \\(P_1\\) the induced one-form wave operator, so\n\\[\nC_gP=P_1C_g.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nk_v=g^{-1}dv=-a^{-1}L_0,\\quad\nK_vp=p(k_v,\\cdot),\\quad\n\\mathcal T=2\\nabla_{k_v}+\\Box_gv.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The constrained hierarchy\n\\[\ni\\mathcal Tp_j+Pp_{j-1}=0,\\qquad\niK_vp_j+C_gp_{j-1}=0,\\qquad p_{-1}=0\n\\]\ncan be constructed with real leading polarization satisfying the whole-footprint lower bound in Proposition 6.</p><p>For every fixed base order \\(s\\), the exterior transfer supplies a real lab-coordinate tensor \\(h^B\\), including the amplitude factor, with\n\\[\n\\|h^B\\|_{C^s}\\le A_T\\lambda^{s+3/2}e^{o(T)}.\n\\]\nIts wave, gauge, and entry-matching residuals can simultaneously be made \\(A_T\\lambda^{-M}e^{o(T)}\\) for any prescribed finite \\(M\\). Increasing accuracy orders does not increase the positive frequency exponent at the fixed base order.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the amplitude-independent constrained construction [22, Proposition 4.6, Corollary 4.8], based on [23, equation (9.5), Propositions 9.6, 9.7].</p><p>On \\(-v\\le u\\le u_f\\), \\(u_f&gt;u_2\\), multiplying the homogeneous transport by \\(-a/2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\nabla_{L_0}+\\tfrac12\\operatorname{tr}_\\gamma\\chi_0.\n\\]\nIn normalized tensor slots its constant diagonal weights are\n\\[\n\\kappa(n_0-n_1)/2.\n\\]\nThe hierarchy is triangular from higher to lower weights. Prescribe nonnegative weights at \\(u=-v\\), fixing components with an \\(L_0\\) argument by gauge; prescribe negative weights \\(1A,11\\) at \\(u_f\\) and transport backward. Each sign is favorable in its prescribed direction. Finite differentiated transport costs only \\(e^{o(T)}\\), including the moving initial edge.</p><p>Orthonormalize a fixed local coframe at entry to obtain \\(e^1,e^2\\), and use the real trace-free unit polarization\n\\[\n2^{-1/2}(e^1\\otimes e^1-e^2\\otimes e^2)\n\\]\ntimes the bump that is at least one on the complete foot set. Higher levels have zero free angular data. The leading transport preserves reality, trace, and annihilation of \\(k_v\\), and acts on the positive screen quotient by isometric parallel transport times a real nonzero scalar. The scalar and reciprocal are \\(e^{o(T)}\\), since its logarithm is an expansion integral. This proves the uniform whole-footprint lower bound.</p><p>Conjugating \\(C_gP=P_1C_g\\) by \\(e^{i\\lambda v}\\) shows that each successive gauge defect satisfies homogeneous one-form transport with zero initial value. Terminal prescriptions for negative weights introduce no additional gauge condition because \\(K_v\\) contracts an \\(L_0\\) argument. Smooth zero extension away from the launch band is allowed.</p><p>The prepared covectors have escaping backward rays and transverse entry, horizon, and initial-slice crossings. The collar has uniform low nondegeneracy; fixed higher coefficient and flow jets are subexponential. Thus the cited exterior transfer applies on the fixed small launch patch. For each thin-annulus tolerance, choose a fixed far threshold and then large \\(T\\).</p><p>The beam superposition integrates over a fixed three-dimensional center patch with normalization \\((\\lambda/2\\pi)^{3/2}\\). Each of \\(s\\) derivatives costs at most \\(\\lambda\\), while finite coefficients after inverse-frequency factors are extracted cost \\(e^{o(T)}\\). Absolute center integration gives the stated norm.</p><p>For the accuracy assertion, let \\(P_c,Q_c\\) be the position and momentum differential blocks of the ray map on the initial complex phase graph, with \\(P_c(0)=\\mathrm{Id}\\), \\(Q_c(0)=M_0\\), \\(\\operatorname{Im}M_0&gt;0\\). The symplectic identity\n\\[\nP_c^*Q_c-Q_c^*P_c=2i\\,\\operatorname{Im}M_0\n\\]\npreserves positive imaginary Hessian through real caustics and supplies subexponential inverse complex projection. At fixed transverse Taylor degree, homogeneous amplitude-jet transport is central transport; returning to fixed coordinates applies the corresponding symmetric power of\n\\[\nJ_c(t,s)^{-t},\\qquad J_c(t,s)=P_c(t)P_c(s)^{-1}.\n\\]\nIts fixed-degree bounds are subexponential. An uncancelled Taylor degree gains \\(\\lambda^{-1/2}e^{o(T)}\\) under Gaussian integration. Increasing finite Taylor and transport orders therefore improves inverse accuracy only. The entry Gaussian expansion is triangular and matches each \\(p_j\\). Linearity permits the amplitude \\(A_T\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Section 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 6 and the beam of Lemma 6.1. In the selected exterior, let \\(t_e\\) be exterior time, \\(z\\) lab position, \\(C(t_e)\\) the drift center, and \\(r_{\\mathrm{co}}=|z-C(t_e)|\\). Let \\(t_T\\asymp T\\) be the launch time and \\(z_*=C(t_T)\\). Choose \\(\\beta&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\ns_*=\\sup|C'|&lt;1-2\\beta,\\qquad\n\\rho_T=(1-2\\beta)t_T.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The beam can be corrected to exact whole-bridge vacuum data, with the support, weighted convergence, tail, and exterior entry accuracy asserted in Proposition 6.</p>", "proof": "<p>The thin beam annulus lies outside the protected ball \\(|z-z_*|&lt;\\rho_T\\) with margin. Set \\(y=(z-z_*)/\\rho_T\\), keeping Cartesian metric components unchanged and multiplying second-fundamental-form components by \\(\\rho_T\\). Both constraints are multiplied by \\(\\rho_T^2\\). A smooth artificial extension of the unperturbed rescaled datum inside the unit ball is \\(O_s(T^{-1})\\) from flat data at every fixed symbol order; no constraint is imposed on that artificial interior.</p><p>For \\(s\\ge3\\), use\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_{H^j_\\ell}\n=\\|f\\|_{H^j(B_2)}\n+\\sup_{R\\ge1}R^\\ell\\|f(R\\cdot)\\|_{H^j(\\{1&lt;|y|&lt;2\\})},\n\\]\n\\[\nX^s=H^{s+2}_1\\oplus H^{s+1}_2,\\qquad Y^s=H^s_4.\n\\]\nThese spaces allow saturated weight-one and weight-two tails. The support-preserving inverse [23, Proposition D.1, Lemma D.2] is\n\\[\n\\mathcal K:Y^s\\to X^s,\\qquad P_{\\mathrm{flat}}\\mathcal Kz=z\n\\]\nfor exterior-supported sources. Its nonlinear contraction requires one finite small base norm; higher bounded symbol norms give smoothness and all-order tails, not higher smallness. Sources may have noncompact tails, and no moment or fixed-charge condition is required.</p><p>Let \\(d_B\\) be the rescaled beam seed and \\(f_B\\) its exterior constraint defect, extended by zero inward. Linearize at the actual rescaled vacuum background on the exterior. Gauss–Codazzi expresses the first variation using the reduced-wave residual and one derivative of the gauge defect. With a fixed base budget,\n\\[\n\\|f_B\\|_{Y^s}\n\\le\\operatorname{poly}(T)e^{o(T)}\n\\left(A_T\\lambda^{-M}+A_T^2\\lambda^{P_*}\\right).\n\\]\nThe seed base norm is\n\\[\nO_s(T^{-1})+\\operatorname{poly}(T)A_T\\lambda^{P_*}e^{o(T)}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\zeta P_*&lt;\\alpha/2\\). The contraction\n\\[\nz=-f_B-\\mathcal N_{d_B}(\\mathcal Kz),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal N_{d_B}(w)=\\Phi(d_B+w)-\\Phi(d_B)-P_{\\mathrm{flat}}w\n\\]\nhas Lipschitz constant at most \\(1/2\\). Its correction is zero on the protected side and has the defect size. Since\n\\[\n\\Phi(d_B+\\mathcal Kz)=\\Phi(d_B)-f_B,\n\\]\nthe exterior constraints vanish. Restoring the original vacuum interior gives smooth exact whole-bridge data.</p><p>The corrected metric remains positive and comparable to the complete original metric. Translation, rescaling, and weighted differentiation cost only powers of \\(T\\). The seed contributes at most\n\\[\n\\operatorname{poly}(T)A_T\\lambda^{m+5/2}e^{o(T)}\n\\]\nto \\(p_m\\); the correction has the corresponding defect bound. Hence \\(p_m(d_T-d)\\to0\\), and every higher seminorm of each individual \\(d_T\\) is finite. The physical tail and charge change are retained.</p><p>Initialize the exact exterior wave-map evolution with target \\(g\\). Use lapse and shift from \\(g+h^B\\); corrected second fundamental form and exact gauge determine the remaining normal jets by a noncharacteristic algebraic system. Their discrepancy has the defect size. Further normal jets follow from the reduced equations.</p><p>For a lab-component change \\(H\\), the reduced equation has the form\n\\[\n\\mathcal R_e(H)_n\n=(\\mathcal L_eH)_n\n+\\bigl((g+H)^{-1}-g^{-1}\\bigr)^{\\rho\\sigma}\\partial_\\rho\\partial_\\sigma H_n\n+\\mathcal Q_n(H,\\partial H),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal Q\\) begins quadratically and has total derivative order at most two. Write \\(H=h^B+F\\), bootstrap high mixed error energies by \\(e^{-\\alpha T}\\), and retain the actual metric in the principal operator. Every remaining nonlinear term has another small packet or error factor. Highest derivatives are placed in \\(L^2\\), lower factors in \\(L^\\infty\\), making the nonlinear rate exponentially small.</p><p>Use shrinking computing slices\n\\[\nr_{\\mathrm{co}}&lt;R_{\\mathrm{out}}(t_e)\n=C_{\\mathrm{out}}(1+T)+V_{\\mathrm{out}}(t_T+C_2-t_e),\n\\qquad\nV_{\\mathrm{out}}&gt;\\sup_{\\mathrm{causal}}|dr_{\\mathrm{co}}/dt_e|.\n\\]\nThe side is strictly outgoing; the full correction tail remains part of the initial datum. The exterior estimate gives exact-order square-root energies\n\\[\nE_j'\\le r_eE_j+C_je^{o(T)}\\sum_{k&lt;j}E_k+\\mathcal F_j,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_j\\le\\operatorname{poly}(T)e^{o(T)}\n(A_T\\lambda^{-M}+A_T^2\\lambda^{P_*}).\n\\]</p><p>The same-order linear rate uses first and second lab metric derivatives, small far out and uniformly bounded elsewhere. Higher derivatives multiply lower exact-order energies. The first-arrival inequality, also valid for the noncompact correction because it vanishes in the protected ball, is\n\\[\n(1-2\\beta)t_T\n\\le(1+\\delta_R)t_e+O(R)+s_*|t_T-t_e|,\n\\qquad \\delta_R\\to0.\n\\]\nThe gap \\(1-s_*&gt;0\\) prevents the change from entering the bounded-rate region before \\(t_e\\ge t_T-\\beta'T\\), where \\(\\beta'\\) can be arbitrarily small. Thus\n\\[\n\\int_0^{t_T+C_2}r_e\\,dt_e\\le\\eta'T\n\\]\nfor any prescribed \\(\\eta'&gt;0\\). These choices also protect \\(v&lt;(1-\\nu)T\\).</p><p>Remove the common rate and iterate the finite lower-triangular hierarchy; this costs a polynomial times \\(e^{o(T)}\\). Choose \\(\\zeta\\) small, then \\(M\\) large, and allocate the losses to obtain\n\\[\n\\|g_T^{\\mathrm{ext}}-g-h^p\\|_{C^{N_{\\mathrm{jet}}}(\\mathcal S)}\n\\le A_T^2e^{\\eta T}\n\\]\nfor an internal \\(\\eta&lt;\\min(\\eta_{\\mathrm{out}},\\alpha/4)\\). Fixed-depth coordinate transitions, finite trace buffers, and entry matching replace \\(h^B\\) by \\(h^p\\). This improves the bootstrap. Local reduced-wave restart inside outflow margins gives the finite exterior; constraint and gauge propagation give exact vacuum.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 6. Let \\(Z=(z^0,z^1,z^\\theta)\\) be the linear variations of optical functions and angles for the input \\(h^p\\), initialized by \\(Z=0\\) on \\(\\mathcal S\\). Write\n\\[\nh^p_{ij}=h^p(L_i,L_j),\\qquad h^p_{iA}=h^p(L_i,\\partial_A).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The linear optical equations are\n\\[\n\\partial_vz^0=-\\frac{h^p_{11}}{2a},\\qquad\nL_0z^1=-\\frac{h^p_{00}}{2a},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\partial_vz^\\theta=b\\,\\partial_vz^0+a\\gamma^{-1}d_\\theta z^0+\\gamma^{-1}h^p_{1\\cdot}.\n\\]\nFor fixed angular order \\(s\\) and total longitudinal order \\(r\\),\n\\[\n|\\partial_{u,v}^r\\partial_\\theta^sZ|\n\\le A_T\\lambda^{r-1}e^{o(T)}.\n\\]</p><p>For the fixed-new-label variation\n\\[\nk=h^p-\\mathcal L_Zg,\n\\]\nlet \\(k_\\gamma,k_H,k_b\\) denote its variations of \\(\\gamma,H=\\log A,b\\). At every fixed required order,\n\\[\n\\|(k_\\gamma,k_H,k_b,q^{-1}\\partial_vk_b)\\|_{C^s}\n\\le A_T\\lambda^{P_s}e^{o(T)},\n\\]\nwhere \\(P_s\\) depends on the base order, not later inverse-accuracy orders.</p>", "proof": "<p>The inverse metric is\n\\[\ng^{-1}=-a^{-1}(L_0\\otimes L_1+L_1\\otimes L_0)\n+\\gamma^{AB}\\partial_A\\otimes\\partial_B.\n\\]\nLinearizing the two eikonal conditions gives the first two equations. Linearizing the shift-free angle condition \\(g^{-1}(du,d\\theta^A)=0\\) gives the third equation, with the displayed signs and factors.</p><p>A typical source for \\(z^0\\) is \\(A_Te^{i\\lambda v}f\\), with fixed coefficient jets \\(e^{o(T)}\\). Integration by parts gives\n\\[\n\\int_{-u}^ve^{i\\lambda s}f(u,s,\\theta)\\,ds\n=\\frac{e^{i\\lambda v}f(u,v,\\theta)-e^{-i\\lambda u}f(u,-u,\\theta)}{i\\lambda}\n-\\frac1{i\\lambda}\\int_{-u}^ve^{i\\lambda s}\\partial_sf\\,ds.\n\\]\nThe interval length is \\(O(T)\\). Longitudinal derivatives cost at most one \\(\\lambda\\) each, including moving-endpoint phases; angular derivatives do not differentiate the phase. The relative source \\(h^p_{11}/a\\) is normalized, so there is no inverse-lapse loss.</p><p>For \\(z^1\\), the leading \\(00\\) coefficient vanishes because \\(p_0\\) annihilates \\(L_0\\); trace reversal does not change the same-null component. Hence \\(h^p_{00}/a\\) already contains \\(\\lambda^{-1}\\). Integrating along \\(L_0\\), with subexponential characteristic jets and moving-edge derivatives, proves the same bound.</p><p>In the angle equation \\(h^p_{1A}\\) is \\(\\sqrt a\\) times a normalized oscillatory coefficient, so the same integration by parts applies. The \\(a\\,d_\\theta z^0\\) term already has the gain. For the remaining term,\n\\[\n\\int_{-u}^vb\\,\\partial_vz^0\\,dv\n=bz^0\\big|_{-u}^v-\\int_{-u}^v(\\partial_vb)z^0\\,dv,\n\\]\nand \\(\\partial_vb\\) retains its factor \\(a\\).</p><p>Set \\(W=z^\\theta-z^0b\\). Direct Lie differentiation gives\n\\[\nk_\\gamma=h^p_{AB}-2z^0\\chi_0-2z^1\\chi_1-\\mathcal L_W\\gamma,\n\\]\n\\[\nk_H=-a^{-1}h^p_{01}-Z\\log a-L_0z^0-\\partial_vz^1,\n\\]\n\\[\nk_b=-\\gamma^{-1}h^p_{0\\cdot}\n+\\partial_uW+[b,W]-z^1\\partial_vb-a\\gamma^{-1}d_\\theta z^1.\n\\]\nThe linear double-null conditions \\(k_{11}=k_{00}=k_{1A}=0\\) follow from the optical equations. Every division by \\(a\\) is relative, giving the three field bounds.</p><p>For the relative shift derivative, let \\(X_i=\\delta\\xi_i\\). Then\n\\[\nX_0+X_1=d_\\theta k_H.\n\\]\nDiffeomorphism covariance gives\n\\[\nD\\operatorname{Ric}_g(\\mathcal L_Zg)=\\mathcal L_Z\\operatorname{Ric}(g)=0.\n\\]\nDeriving linear null identities may lose fixed inverse powers of \\(q\\), but only on the linear residual. On \\(\\mathcal D_T\\) these cost \\(e^{C_sT}\\), absorbed by increasing \\(M\\) after \\(\\zeta\\) is fixed.</p><p>The direction-\\(1\\) torsion equation linearizes to\n\\[\n(\\partial_v+\\tau_1)X_0\n=\\delta\\beta_1+\\delta\\tau_1\\,\\xi_1+\\tau_1d_\\theta k_H+r_{\\mathrm{lin}},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\delta\\chi_1=\\tfrac12\\partial_vk_\\gamma,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\delta\\tau_1=\\operatorname{tr}_\\gamma\\delta\\chi_1\n-\\gamma^{AC}k_{\\gamma,CD}\\gamma^{DB}\\chi_{1,AB},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\delta\\beta_1\n=\\operatorname{div}_\\gamma\\delta\\chi_1-d_\\theta\\delta\\tau_1\n+(\\delta\\operatorname{div}_\\gamma)\\chi_1.\n\\]\nThe source contains neither unknown \\(X_i\\) nor an unknown relative shift derivative. Its entry value is a finite jet at \\(q=q_0\\), and\n\\[\n\\int_{-u}^v|\\tau_1|\\,ds\\le C.\n\\]\nIntegrating factors give the required bound for \\(X_0\\), then \\(X_1\\). Commutation leaves the same top transport coefficient; differentiated coefficients multiply lower jets, moving-entry terms are controlled, and \\(v\\)-derivatives follow from the equation.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nq^{-1}\\partial_vk_b\n=A\\gamma^{-1}(X_1-X_0)\n+(k_H\\mathrm{Id}-\\gamma^{-1}k_\\gamma)q^{-1}\\partial_vb.\n\\]\nThis proves the relative estimate without dividing an ordinary error bound by small \\(q\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 5.1, used as an intermediate result for Theorem 1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the background and branch tube of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix finite \\(m,s\\), enlarging \\(s\\) if necessary so \\(s\\ge30\\), and a sufficiently small fixed \\(c&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\n\\Omega_T=\n\\left\\{\n0\\le t\\le L_T,\\quad\nu=t-x\\le u_2-c_0t/T,\\quad\nv=t+x\\le T+c_2-c_0t/T,\\quad\n\\theta\\in S^2\n\\right\\},\n\\]\nwith the construction margins and positive lower slice-length bound.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the common \\(q=q_0e^{-\\kappa(u+v)}\\), \\(\\sigma=\\sqrt q\\), \\(\\alpha=\\kappa/4\\), \\(A_T=e^{-\\alpha T}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Undecorated fields are background fields, barred fields are the exact double-null template of Proposition 6, and primed fields are the actual vacuum solution.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nU=(\\gamma,\\log A,b,\\chi_0,\\chi_1,\\ell_0,\\ell_1,\\xi_0,\\xi_1).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The preparation parameters can be chosen so that the actual entry data have a smooth vacuum development on \\(\\Omega_T\\), in the double-null gauge initialized there. The relative lapse and sphere metric stay positive and uniformly nondegenerate, and\n\\[\n\\max_{|I|\\le s}\\|\\partial^I(U'-\\bar U)\\|_{L^\\infty(\\Omega_T)}\n\\le e^{-(1+c)\\alpha T}.\n\\]\nThe same holds for\n\\[\nq^{-1}\\partial_vb'-q^{-1}\\partial_v\\bar b\n\\]\nat any preassigned finite order after enlarging the order budget.</p><p>The individual ordinary jets of the primed relative fields through every order used in the experiment have uniform \\(e^{o(T)}\\) bounds. Both the solution and template equal the background where \\(v&lt;(1-\\nu)T\\). The exact solution agrees on an entry collar, by local geometric uniqueness, with the actual exterior solution.</p>", "proof": "<p>The equations are those in Proposition 6. Their angular system follows by applying divergence minus trace differential to the cross-shape equation and using\n\\[\nL_iK_\\gamma=\\operatorname{div}\\beta_i-\\tau_iK_\\gamma,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_i\\operatorname{div}\\zeta\n=\\operatorname{div}(\\mathcal L_i\\zeta)\n-2\\chi_i^{AB}\\nabla_A\\zeta_B\n-(2\\operatorname{div}\\chi_i-d\\tau_i)\\cdot\\zeta,\n\\]\n\\[\nL_i\\operatorname{curl}\\xi_j\n=\\operatorname{curl}\\beta_i+\\operatorname{curl}(\\tau_i\\xi_i)\n-\\tau_i\\operatorname{curl}\\xi_j.\n\\]\nIn particular divergence of \\(\\beta_i\\) cancels from \\(L_im_i\\), whose explicit source is\n\\[\n-\\tau_iK_\\gamma-\\operatorname{div}(\\tau_i\\xi_i)\n+2\\chi_i^{AB}\\nabla_A(\\xi_j)_B\n+(2\\operatorname{div}\\chi_i-d\\tau_i)\\cdot\\xi_j.\n\\]\nThe normalized trace equation preserves the constant damping \\(\\kappa\\).</p><p>Choose a spatial target \\(k\\ge2s\\), enlarged for bootstrap and continuation norms. Choose a slice Sobolev order \\(M\\) so large that, with \\(\\vartheta=(k+2)/M\\),\n\\[\n2\\alpha(1-\\vartheta)&gt;(1+c_1)\\alpha,\n\\qquad 0&lt;c&lt;c_1&lt;1/2.\n\\]\nLemma 7.1 determines a finite entry buffer \\(r(M)\\). Include this, the template and exterior requirements, and \\(m\\) in the base budget. Choose \\(\\eta_2\\) small enough to retain a strict interpolation gap. In Lemma 7.2 choose multipliers, thresholds, growth tolerance, support fraction, and then input losses.</p><p>Let \\(P_{\\mathrm{base}}\\) dominate all fixed positive frequency exponents. Choose \\(\\zeta\\) small, in particular \\(\\zeta P_{\\mathrm{base}}&lt;\\alpha/2\\). Only afterward enlarge hierarchy and beam accuracy to absorb linear inverse-lapse losses. Proposition 6 ensures that this does not increase the budgeted positive powers. Then take \\(T\\) large.</p><p>Local smooth vacuum theory and the noncharacteristic optical transports start the solution. On every existing bootstrap slice, Lemmas 7.1–7.2 give for each component error \\(f\\)\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_{L^2}\\le e^{-(2\\alpha-\\eta_2)T},\n\\qquad\n\\|f\\|_{H^M}\\le e^{o(T)}.\n\\]\nThe template has the same individual high bounds by its construction. Sphere charts and interval extension yield\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_{C^k(I_T(t)\\times S^2)}\n\\le C\\operatorname{poly}(T)\\|f\\|_{L^2}^{1-\\vartheta}\n\\|f\\|_{H^M}^{\\vartheta}\n\\le e^{-(1+c_1)\\alpha T},\n\\]\nafter reducing \\(c_1\\) within its margin. The positive lower interval length gives uniform local extension constants; longer intervals cost at most a polynomial. No lower bound for the available time thickness is needed.</p><p>Recover time derivatives from\n\\[\n\\partial_t\\gamma=4\\chi_1-\\partial_x\\gamma,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\partial_t\\log A=2(\\ell_1+\\kappa)-\\partial_x\\log A,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\partial_tb=2qA\\gamma^{-1}(\\xi_1-\\xi_0)-\\partial_xb,\n\\]\nand the cross-shape, cross-lapse, and torsion equations. Direction \\(0\\) uses\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=(\\partial_t-\\partial_x)/2+\\mathcal L_b.\n\\]\nA time derivative requires at most two spatial derivatives. Thus spatial order \\(k\\ge2s\\) covers total ordinary order \\(s\\). Subtraction with actual operators, finite defects, and individual high bounds introduce no division by \\(q\\); allocate arbitrarily small rate losses to retain \\(c&lt;c_1\\). The relative shift estimate follows algebraically.</p><p>The template change at bootstrap orders is \\(e^{-(\\alpha-\\eta_0)T}\\), \\(\\eta_0&lt;\\alpha/2\\). Together with the error estimate it is less than \\(\\frac12e^{-\\alpha T/2}\\), improving the bootstrap and preserving positivity.</p><p>For continuation, at each fixed \\(T\\),\n\\[\nq\\ge q_0e^{-2\\kappa L_T}&gt;0.\n\\]\nAll required finite norms and inverse metrics stay bounded up to a proposed terminal slice. Restart from an earlier slice in wave gauge, extending smooth slice jets over enlarged interval margins. Constraints are needed only on the original margin. Reduced local existence and optical transport give a positive restart time at this fixed \\(T\\). Restrict to the outgoing-side region: \\(du,dv\\ge0\\) prevents artificial extension data from entering. Constraint and gauge propagation give vacuum; geometric uniqueness and optical uniqueness identify overlaps. Restart contradicts termination before the full slab.</p><p>At fixed \\(T\\), the finite-region high-current estimate propagates every larger finite order from smooth entry data, so the solution is smooth on the whole domain. Collar uniqueness identifies it with the actual exterior solution. No restart-time lower bound uniform in \\(T\\) is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 7 on any existing initial partial slab. Bootstrap\n\\[\n\\|U'-U\\|_{C^{10}}\\le e_T,\\qquad e_T=e^{-\\alpha T/2},\n\\]\nand keep the relative lapse and sphere metric in a fixed open nondegeneracy range. The low fields are majorized by\n\\[\nh_{i,T}=C_0(q+g_i(y_i)+e_T),\n\\]\nincluding the low pure longitudinal jets.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed finite order \\(n\\), there is a finite initial order \\(r(n)\\) such that uniform \\(e^{o(T)}\\) initial bounds through \\(r(n)\\) imply uniform \\(e^{o(T)}\\) bounds through order \\(n\\) for all ordinary jets of\n\\[\n\\gamma'^{\\pm1},\\ \\log A',\\ b',\\ \\chi_i',\\ \\ell_i',\\ \\xi_i',\\ q^{-1}\\partial_vb'.\n\\]\nConstants depend only on the orders, a prescribed rate tolerance, the bounded low coefficient set, uniform envelope bounds, and finitely many initial norms. They do not depend on uncontrolled high norms or on the partial-slab endpoint.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose smooth cutoffs \\(\\psi_{i,T}\\), equal to one on the coordinate projections and supported in \\([-C(T+1),C(T+1)]\\), and extend only the scalar envelopes:\n\\[\nG_{i,T}(y)=g_i(y)+e_T\\psi_{i,T}(y),\\qquad h_i=C_0(q+G_{i,T}).\n\\]\nThe added \\(L^1\\) mass and sum of unit-cell suprema are \\(O((T+1)e_T)\\), uniformly bounded and tending to zero. Their tails are uniformly tight because\n\\[\n\\sup_T\\int_{|y|&gt;R}e_T\\psi_{i,T}(y)\\,dy\n\\le C\\sup_{T+1\\ge R/C}(T+1)e^{-\\alpha T/2}\\to0.\n\\]\nThe same controls tail suprema. The Lipschitz potential\n\\[\n\\Phi_T=C_0\\sum_{i=0}^1\\int_{-\\infty}^{y_i}G_{i,T}(r)\\,dr\n+\\frac{C_0q_0}{\\kappa}(1-e^{-\\kappa(u+v)})\n\\]\nsatisfies \\(0\\le\\Phi_T\\le C\\) and \\(L_i'\\Phi_T=h_i\\) almost everywhere. Both directional integrals are bounded.</p><p>Suppress primes in this proof. Fix a time-independent reference connection \\(D\\) on \\(S^2\\), and set\n\\[\n|f|_k^2=\\sum_{r=0}^k\\int_{S^2}|D^rf|_\\gamma^2\\,d\\mu_\\gamma.\n\\]\nUniform low metric bounds make these equivalent to fixed-reference Sobolev norms. In particular, \\(|\\gamma|_k\\) records coordinate coefficient jets.</p><p>For \\(M\\ge5\\), use\n\\[\nY_i=|\\beta_i|_M,\\quad\nZ_i=\\sigma|(Q_i,m_i)|_M,\\quad\nR_i=|\\chi_i|_{M+1},\\quad\nz_i=|\\tau_i/A|_{M+1},\n\\]\n\\[\nd_i=|B_i|_{M+1},\\quad d_1=0,\\quad D_b=d_0,\n\\]\n\\[\nP_\\gamma=1+|\\gamma|_{M+1},\\quad\nP_i=|\\xi_j|_M,\\quad P=P_\\gamma+P_0+P_1.\n\\]\nTame inversion and \\(d\\log A=\\xi_0+\\xi_1\\) bound inverse metrics and relative lapse factors by \\(C_MP\\). The low \\(A^{\\pm1}\\) bounds control the mean of \\(\\log A\\).</p><p>The angular elliptic estimates are\n\\[\nR_i\\le C_M(Y_i+z_i+h_iP),\n\\qquad\n|\\xi_j|_{M+1}\\le C_M(\\sigma^{-1}Z_i+P).\n\\]\nFor the first, use the trace-free tensor\n\\[\nS_i=\\chi_i-\\tfrac12\\tau_i\\gamma,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{div}S_i=\\beta_i+\\tfrac12d\\tau_i.\n\\]\nFor the second, use\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}\\xi_j=K_\\gamma-m_i,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{curl}\\xi_j=c_i.\n\\]\nFreeze principal coefficients on small fixed charts and use metric-dependent trace-free bases. Differentiated coefficient errors are tame:\n\\[\nC_M(|f|_M+|\\gamma|_{M+1}\\|f\\|_{C^1}).\n\\]\nInterpolation absorbs the penultimate derivative. High metric coefficients remain variables in \\(P\\), not constants.</p><p>Commute the angular system with \\(D^r\\), transporting all derivative slots by \\(\\mathcal L_i\\). Pair the new gradient and one-form slots using\n\\[\n(\\nabla F)_{IA}=\\nabla_AF_I,\\quad\n(\\operatorname{div}B)_I=\\gamma^{AB}\\nabla_AB_{IB},\\quad\n(\\operatorname{curl}B)_I=\\epsilon_\\gamma^{AB}\\nabla_AB_{IB}.\n\\]\nUse currents\n\\[\ne_{Y_i}=\\frac12\\sum_{r=0}^M|D^r\\beta_i|_\\gamma^2\n\\quad\\text{in direction }j,\n\\]\n\\[\ne_{Z_i}=\\frac a4\\sum_{r=0}^M\n\\bigl(|D^rQ_i|_\\gamma^2+|D^rm_i|_\\gamma^2\\bigr)\n\\quad\\text{in direction }i.\n\\]\nTheir principal angular terms cancel on the full sphere except\n\\[\n-\\frac12\\int_{S^2}\n\\langle D^r\\beta_i,(da)D^rK_\\gamma+J(da)D^rc_i\\rangle_\\gamma\\,d\\mu_\\gamma,\n\\]\nbounded by \\(C_M\\sigma Y_iZ_i\\), since \\(da=a(\\xi_0+\\xi_1)\\).</p><p>The connection difference is\n\\[\n(\\nabla-D)^C{}_{AB}\n=\\tfrac12\\gamma^{CD}(D_A\\gamma_{BD}+D_B\\gamma_{AD}-D_D\\gamma_{AB}).\n\\]\nRepresentative commutator bounds are\n\\[\n|D^r\\{a(dK_\\gamma+Jdc_i)\\}\n-a\\{\\nabla D^rK_\\gamma+J\\nabla D^rc_i\\}|_0\n\\le C_M(\\sigma Z_i+qP),\n\\]\n\\[\n|D^r\\{(\\operatorname{div},\\operatorname{curl})\\beta_i\\}\n-(\\operatorname{div},\\operatorname{curl})D^r\\beta_i|_0\n\\le C_M(Y_i+h_iP),\n\\]\n\\[\n|[D^r,\\mathcal L_j]\\beta_i|_0\n\\le C_M(h_jY_i+h_id_j).\n\\]\nHigh angular coefficients multiply low \\(dQ_i\\), \\(\\beta_i\\), or \\(d\\beta_i\\); the scalar-current weight supplies the additional \\(\\sigma\\). Lie commutators use low \\(DB_j\\) against top jets or high \\(B_j\\) against low fields. The scalar commutators cost \\(C_M(\\sigma d_i+h_iZ_i)\\), trace commutators \\(C_Mh_i(z_i+d_i)\\), and torsion commutators \\(C_M(h_iP_i+d_i)\\).</p><p>Every torsion derivative in an \\(a\\)-term satisfies\n\\[\nq(|\\xi_0|_{M+1}+|\\xi_1|_{M+1})\n\\le C_M\\{\\sigma(Z_0+Z_1)+qP\\}.\n\\]\nTogether with tame products, the paired squared-current costs are bounded by\n\\[\nC_MY_i\\bigl[\n\\sigma(Z_0+Z_1+P)+h_j(Y_i+R_i)\n+h_i(R_j+d_j)+h_ih_jP\n\\bigr],\n\\]\n\\[\nC_MZ_i\\bigl[\n\\sigma(Y_i+R_i+d_i+P)+h_i(Z_0+Z_1)\n\\bigr].\n\\]\nThe trace, shift, metric, and torsion currents have costs\n\\[\nC_Mz_ih_i(z_i+R_i+d_i+h_iP),\n\\]\n\\[\nC_MD_b[\\sigma(Z_0+Z_1+\\sigma P)+h_1D_b],\n\\]\n\\[\nC_MP_\\gamma R_1+C_Mh_1P_\\gamma^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nC_MP_i(Y_i+R_i+d_i+h_iP).\n\\]\nArea and norm differentiation gives only low shapes; differentiating \\(a\\) gives favorable \\(-\\kappa\\) damping plus \\(O(h_i)\\).</p><p>On a fixed finite slab, substitution of the elliptic estimates and multiplication by \\(e^{-2Kt}\\), with \\(K\\) larger than the bounded low coefficient constant, gives finite flux bounds. Thus all estimates are a priori estimates on existing slabs.</p><p>For an arbitrarily small exponential rate, fix \\(0&lt;\\delta\\le1\\). At \\(\\sigma=h_0=h_1=0\\), the only nonzero arrows are\n\\[\nY_i,z_i\\longrightarrow P_i,\\qquad\nD_b\\longrightarrow P_0,\\qquad\nY_1,z_1\\longrightarrow P_\\gamma.\n\\]\nThere is an asymmetric regime: give along-\\(0\\) variables \\(Y_1,Z_0,z_0,P_0\\) arbitrarily large damping and remove products containing them. At \\(\\sigma=h_1=0\\), with bounded \\(h_0\\), the remaining arrows are\n\\[\nz_1\\longrightarrow Y_0,\\quad\nz_1\\longrightarrow P_1,\\quad\nz_1\\longrightarrow P_\\gamma.\n\\]\nAssign paired multipliers one, trace multipliers \\(\\Lambda^2\\), and \\(P\\)-multipliers \\(\\epsilon_P^2\\). Choosing \\(\\Lambda\\) large and \\(\\epsilon_P\\) small makes both limiting forms have norm at most \\(\\delta/4\\). Continuity gives a fixed small threshold \\(\\epsilon_*\\).</p><p>Uniform envelope tails provide \\(B_{\\mathrm{cut}},S_0\\), independent of large \\(T\\), so the all-small regime holds for \\(t&gt;S_0,u&lt;-B_{\\mathrm{cut}}\\), and the asymmetric regime holds for \\(t&gt;S_0,-B_{\\mathrm{cut}}\\le u\\le u_2\\). Use\n\\[\nw_T=\\exp\\{-2\\delta(u+v)-2K_0(u+B_{\\mathrm{cut}})_+\n-2K_0\\min(t,S_0)\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n-L_k\\log w_T\n=2\\delta+2K_0\\mathbf1_{\\{k=0,u&gt;-B_{\\mathrm{cut}}\\}}\n+K_0\\mathbf1_{\\{t&lt;S_0\\}}\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. Choose \\(K_0&gt;C(C_{\\mathrm{bd}}+C_{\\mathrm{bd}}^2/\\delta)\\). The choice order is\n\\[\n(\\Lambda,\\epsilon_P),\\quad \\epsilon_*,\\quad\n(B_{\\mathrm{cut}},S_0),\\quad K_0,\n\\]\nso changing \\(K_0\\) does not alter the tail threshold.</p><p>Integration yields positive null and slice fluxes, including on the tilted outgoing sides. Removing the weight loses only an arbitrarily small exponential rate, because \\(u\\le u_2\\), \\(v\\le C(T+1)\\). Initial interval integration costs a polynomial. In particular,\n\\[\n\\sup_{y_j}\\|Y_i+d_i\\|_{L^2(dy_i)}\n+\\sup_{y_i}\\|Z_i\\|_{L^2(dy_j)}\n\\le e^{o(T)}.\n\\]</p><p>Recover unweighted fields by transport, not by dividing \\(Z_i\\) by \\(\\sigma\\). The damped trace equation gives\n\\[\nz_i(y)\\le e^{o(T)}e^{-\\kappa(y_i+y_j)}\n+C'_M\\int_{-y_j}^{y_i}\ne^{-\\kappa(y_i-r)}h_i(Y_i+d_i+h_iP)(r,y_j)\\,dr.\n\\]\nIntegrating in the terminal variable, reversing the triangle, and using transverse flux and \\(h_i^2\\le Ch_i\\) gives\n\\[\n\\int_{-y_j}^{y_i}(z_i+R_i)(r,y_j)\\,dr\n\\le e^{o(T)}\n+C_M\\int_{-y_j}^{y_i}h_iP(r,y_j)\\,dr.\n\\]\nMetric and torsion transport then imply\n\\[\nP(y)\\le e^{o(T)}\n+C''_M\\sum_i\\int_{-y_j}^{y_i}h_iP(r,y_j)\\,dr.\n\\]\nMultiply by \\(e^{-C\\Phi_T}\\), use\n\\[\n\\int_{-y_j}^{y_i}h_i(r,y_j)\ne^{-C(\\Phi_T(y)-\\Phi_T(r,y_j))}\\,dr\\le C^{-1},\n\\]\nand choose \\(C&gt;4C''_M\\). This gives pointwise \\(P,z_i\\le e^{o(T)}\\), and the preceding integral bounds for \\(R_i\\).</p><p>The cross equations now give pointwise \\(H^{M-1}\\) bounds for \\(\\chi_i,\\ell_i\\): their angular sources have size \\(C_MqP\\), and remaining terms are integrable using the transverse fluxes and \\(R_j\\)-integrals. The shift equation gives pointwise \\(H^M\\) bounds for \\(b\\), using pointwise torsion bounds. Running \\(M\\) high enough gives any fixed angular target.</p><p>For longitudinal derivatives, fix \\(n_\\parallel\\) and \\(A_{\\mathrm{out}}\\ge5\\), and set\n\\[\nA_k=A_{\\mathrm{out}}+2(n_\\parallel-k).\n\\]\nInduct on ordered transport words of length \\(k\\), retaining the larger buffers at lower levels. The identities are\n\\[\n[\\mathcal L_j,\\mathcal L_i]=\\mathcal L_{X_{ij}},\n\\qquad X_{ij}=a(\\xi_j-\\xi_i)^\\sharp,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_i\\gamma=2\\chi_i,\\qquad\nL_i\\log A=\\ell_i+\\kappa,\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_i(\\xi_0+\\xi_1)=d\\ell_i.\n\\]\nThe reductions\n\\[\nL_i^k\\tau_i=L_i^{k-1}(\\ell_i\\tau_i-|\\chi_i|^2),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_i^k\\xi_j=\\mathcal L_i^{k-1}(\\beta_i+\\tau_i\\xi_i),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_i^k\\xi_i=dL_i^{k-1}\\ell_i-\\mathcal L_i^k\\xi_j,\n\\]\n\\[\nL_i^kK_\\gamma=L_i^{k-1}(\\operatorname{div}\\beta_i-\\tau_iK_\\gamma),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n[\\mathcal L_j,\\mathcal L_i^k]\n=\\sum_{r=0}^{k-1}\\mathcal L_i^r\\mathcal L_{X_{ij}}\\mathcal L_i^{k-1-r}\n\\]\nadd at most two angular derivatives per reduction. The angular connection variation is\n\\[\n(\\mathcal L_i\\nabla)^C{}_{AB}\n=\\gamma^{CD}(\\nabla_A\\chi_{i,BD}\n+\\nabla_B\\chi_{i,AD}-\\nabla_D\\chi_{i,AB}).\n\\]</p><p>For \\(U_{i,k}=\\mathcal L_i^k\\chi_i\\), \\(V_{i,k}=L_i^k\\ell_i\\), commuting the cross equations gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_jU_{i,k}\n=-\\tfrac12\\tau_jU_{i,k}+U_{i,k}\\chi_j+\\chi_jU_{i,k}+F_{i,k},\n\\]\n\\[\nL_jV_{i,k}=-U_{i,k}\\cdot\\chi_j+G_{i,k},\n\\]\nwith sources from lower longitudinal levels and buffered angular derivatives. In particular, the differentiated trace term reduces to lower levels, and the new lapse jet is absent from the top right side.</p><p>Commuting in increasing angular order gives top coefficient \\(C_rh_j\\); high coefficients multiply already controlled lower angular orders. Thus Gronwall exponentiates only a bounded directional integral, while finite products and interval integration preserve \\(e^{o(T)}\\). Mixed words reduce by commuting a cross derivative inward and using shorter commutator words.</p><p>For \\(b\\), differentiate the direction-\\(1\\) equation in \\(u\\) and integrate. Converting \\(\\partial_u=L_0-\\mathcal L_b\\) uses only lower longitudinal \\(b\\)-orders and fits the same angular buffer. Mixed shift jets follow from its equation, and\n\\[\nq^{-1}\\partial_vb=A\\gamma^{-1}(\\xi_1-\\xi_0)\n\\]\nis algebraic.</p><p>All required entry jets are finite. They may be expressed using tangential entry jets and the cross equations, where \\(q=q_0&gt;0\\). Taking \\(n_\\parallel=n\\), \\(A_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\max(n,5)\\), and a sufficiently large angular current order determines a finite \\(r(n)\\). Only low directional integrals enter exponentials; high norms enter polynomially. This proves the uniform dependence claimed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Proposition 5.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the bootstrap setting of Lemma 7.1. Write \\(\\Delta f=f'-\\bar f\\), and assume the entry discrepancies and required equation defects are bounded through a fixed finite budget by\n\\[\nF_T=A_T^2e^{\\eta T}.\n\\]\nAll differences vanish for \\(v&lt;(1-\\nu)T\\).</p><p>On each sphere define\n\\[\nY_i=|\\Delta\\beta_i|_0,\\quad\nZ_i=\\sigma|(\\Delta Q_i,\\Delta m_i)|_0,\\quad\nz_i=|\\Delta(\\tau_i/A)|_1,\n\\]\n\\[\nN_i=|\\Delta\\chi_i|_0+|\\Delta\\ell_i|_0,\\quad\nD_b=|\\Delta b|_1,\\quad\nR_i=|\\Delta\\chi_i|_1,\n\\]\n\\[\nP=|\\Delta\\gamma|_1+|\\Delta\\log A|_0\n+|\\Delta\\xi_0|_0+|\\Delta\\xi_1|_0,\n\\]\n\\[\nV=P+D_b+\\sum_i(N_i+Y_i+Z_i+z_i),\n\\qquad H_s=\\sigma+h_0+h_1.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\eta_2&gt;0\\), choose the input loss \\(\\eta\\) and support fraction \\(\\nu\\) sufficiently small, with the base orders fixed. On every existing bootstrap subslab,\n\\[\n\\sup_t\\|(\\Delta\\gamma,\\Delta\\log A,\\Delta b,\n\\Delta\\chi_i,\\Delta\\ell_i,\\Delta\\xi_i)\\|_{L^2(I_T(t)\\times S^2)}\n\\le A_T^2e^{\\eta_2T},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\|Y_i+Z_i+z_i\\|_{L^2(I_T(t))}\n+\\|(\\nabla\\Delta\\gamma,\\nabla\\Delta b)\\|_{L^2(I_T(t)\\times S^2)}\n\\le A_T^2e^{\\eta_2T}.\n\\]\nThe supremum is restricted to available slices.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(h_{AB}=\\Delta\\gamma_{AB}\\). The exact angular connection difference is\n\\[\nC^C{}_{AB}\n=\\tfrac12(\\gamma'^{-1})^{CD}\n(\\bar\\nabla_Ah_{BD}+\\bar\\nabla_Bh_{AD}-\\bar\\nabla_Dh_{AB}),\n\\]\nso \\(|C|_0\\le C|\\Delta\\gamma|_1\\). For a barred one-form \\(f\\),\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{div}'-\\overline{\\operatorname{div}})f\n=((\\gamma'^{-1})^{AB}-(\\bar\\gamma^{-1})^{AB})\\bar\\nabla_Af_B\n-(\\gamma'^{-1})^{AB}C^D{}_{AB}f_D,\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{curl}'-\\overline{\\operatorname{curl}})f\n=\\tfrac12((\\epsilon')^{AB}-\\bar\\epsilon^{AB})(df)_{AB}.\n\\]\nThere is no second derivative of \\(h\\). On \\(\\bar\\beta_i,\\bar\\chi_i\\) these cost \\(Ch_iP\\); on bounded torsion they cost \\(CP\\).</p><p>The torsion sum and normalized trace identities give\n\\[\n|\\Delta\\log A|_1+|\\Delta A|_1\\le CP,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta\\tau_i=A'\\Delta(\\tau_i/A)+(\\Delta A)\\bar w_i,\n\\qquad\n|\\Delta\\tau_i|_1\\le C(z_i+h_iP).\n\\]\nWith \\(C_{\\mathrm{inv}}=\\gamma'^{-1}-\\bar\\gamma^{-1}\\), the shape Hodge datum is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}'\\Delta\\chi_i-d\\operatorname{tr}'\\Delta\\chi_i\n=\\Delta\\beta_i-(\\operatorname{div}'-\\overline{\\operatorname{div}})\\bar\\chi_i\n+d(C_{\\mathrm{inv}}:\\bar\\chi_i).\n\\]\nThe trace-free divergence estimate, including the undifferentiated norm for its kernel, and the one-form div–curl estimate give\n\\[\nR_i\\le C(Y_i+N_i+z_i+h_iP),\n\\qquad\n|\\Delta\\xi_j|_1\\le C(\\sigma^{-1}Z_i+P).\n\\]\nThe latter uses\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}'\\Delta\\xi_j\n=\\Delta K_\\gamma-\\Delta m_i\n-(\\operatorname{div}'-\\overline{\\operatorname{div}})\\bar\\xi_j,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{curl}'\\Delta\\xi_j\n=\\Delta c_i-(\\operatorname{curl}'-\\overline{\\operatorname{curl}})\\bar\\xi_j.\n\\]\nIt does not give an unweighted torsion-derivative bound by \\(Z_i\\).</p><p>Keep the actual transport operator:\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_i'f'-\\bar{\\mathcal L}_i\\bar f\n=\\mathcal L_i'\\Delta f+\\mathcal L_{\\Delta B_i}\\bar f.\n\\]\nThe shift difference costs \\(Ch_iD_b\\) on barred shapes and \\(\\beta_i\\), \\(CD_b\\) on torsion and scalar curvature fields, and \\(Ch_iD_b\\) on \\(\\bar\\ell_i\\), since its constant \\(-\\kappa\\) has zero angular derivative.</p><p>The paired principal difference system, with \\(k=\\Delta K_\\gamma\\), \\(c=\\Delta c_i\\), is\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_j'\\Delta\\beta_i=\\frac{a'}2(dk+J'dc)+F_i,\n\\]\n\\[\nL_i'\\Delta Q_i=(\\operatorname{div}',\\operatorname{curl}')\\Delta\\beta_i+G_i,\n\\qquad\nL_i'\\Delta m_i=G_i^m.\n\\]\nUse densities\n\\[\ne_{Y_i}=\\tfrac12|\\Delta\\beta_i|_{\\gamma'}^2,\n\\qquad\ne_{Z_i}=\\frac{a'}4(|\\Delta Q_i|^2+|\\Delta m_i|^2).\n\\]\nTheir principal angular terms cancel except\n\\[\n-\\tfrac12\\int_{S^2}\n\\langle\\Delta\\beta_i,(da')k+J'(da')c\\rangle_{\\gamma'}\\,d\\mu_{\\gamma'},\n\\]\nbounded by \\(C\\sigma Y_iZ_i\\). Differentiating the scalar weight gives favorable \\(-\\kappa\\) damping; area and norm variation costs \\(C(h_jY_i^2+h_iZ_i^2)\\).</p><p>The principal-coefficient change on barred fields is\n\\[\n\\tfrac12\\{(a'-\\bar a)(d\\bar K+\\bar Jd\\bar c_i)\n+a'(J'-\\bar J)d\\bar c_i\\},\n\\]\nof size \\(CqP\\). Divergence/curl differences on \\(\\bar\\beta_i\\) cost \\(Ch_iP\\), with a further \\(\\sigma\\) after weighting. Other \\(a\\)-terms cost \\(C\\sigma(Z_0+Z_1+P)\\). Shape products cost\n\\[\nC(h_iR_j+h_jR_i+h_ih_jP).\n\\]\nThe weighted scalar remainders cost\n\\[\nC\\{\\sigma(R_i+D_b+P)+h_i(Z_0+Z_1)\\}.\n\\]\nEvery dangerous torsion derivative occurs only as\n\\[\na'|\\Delta\\xi|_1\\le C(\\sigma Z+qP),\n\\qquad\n\\sigma h_i|\\Delta\\xi|_1\\le C(h_iZ+\\sigma h_iP),\n\\quad Z=Z_0+Z_1.\n\\]\nThus the combined paired cost is\n\\[\nCH_s(Y_i+Z_i)(V+R_0+R_1),\n\\]\napart from defects.</p><p>For the trace, subtract\n\\[\n(L_i'+\\kappa)\\Delta w_i\n=-\\Delta(A^{-1}|\\chi_i|^2)-\\Delta B_i\\cdot d\\bar w_i+F_i^w.\n\\]\nOne angular commutation costs \\(Ch_i(z_i+R_i+D_b+h_iP)\\), in addition to the favorable constant damping. No \\(\\Delta\\ell_i\\) is generated.</p><p>The shape and lapse cross-equation source factors are\n\\[\na\\nabla\\xi,\\qquad aK_\\gamma,\\qquad a\\xi^2,\\qquad \\chi_i*\\chi_j.\n\\]\nTheir differences are bounded by\n\\[\nC\\{\\sigma(Z_0+Z_1)+qP+h_iN_j+h_jN_i+h_ih_jP+h_iD_b\\}.\n\\]\nFor example,\n\\[\n\\Delta(a\\operatorname{Sym}\\nabla\\xi_j)\n=a'\\operatorname{Sym}\\nabla'\\Delta\\xi_j\n+(\\Delta a)\\operatorname{Sym}\\bar\\nabla\\bar\\xi_j\n-a'\\operatorname{Sym}(C*\\bar\\xi_j),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta(aK_\\gamma\\gamma)\n=a'(\\Delta K_\\gamma)\\gamma'\n+a'\\bar K_\\gamma\\Delta\\gamma+(\\Delta a)\\bar K_\\gamma\\bar\\gamma.\n\\]\nThe squared \\(N_i\\)-current cost is therefore at most\n\\[\nCH_sN_i(V+R_0+R_1).\n\\]</p><p>For the \\(H^1\\) shift current,\n\\[\n\\partial_v\\Delta b=q\\,\\Delta\\{A\\gamma^{-1}(\\xi_1-\\xi_0)\\},\n\\]\nand one angular derivative costs \\(C(\\sigma(Z_0+Z_1)+qP)\\). Its squared cost is \\(CH_sD_bV\\).</p><p>Recover \\(\\Delta\\gamma\\) in \\(H^1\\) and \\(\\Delta H\\) in \\(L^2\\), \\(H=\\log A\\), in direction \\(1\\):\n\\[\n\\partial_v\\Delta\\gamma=2\\Delta\\chi_1,\\qquad\n\\partial_v\\Delta H=\\Delta\\ell_1.\n\\]\nFor torsion,\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_i'\\Delta\\xi_j\n=\\Delta\\beta_i+\\tau_i'\\Delta\\xi_i\n+(\\Delta\\tau_i)\\bar\\xi_i-\\mathcal L_{\\Delta B_i}\\bar\\xi_j+F_i^\\xi.\n\\]\nIn the undifferentiated norm use \\(|\\Delta\\tau_i|_0\\le C(N_i+h_iP)\\). Fixed-background angular norms avoid unnecessary time differentiation. The total recovery cost is\n\\[\nCP\\left\\{\\sum_i(Y_i+N_i+R_i)+D_b+H_sP\\right\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Substitute the shape Hodge estimate. At \\(H_s=0\\), the only unfavorable arrows are\n\\[\n(Y_i,N_i,z_i,D_b)\\longrightarrow P.\n\\]\nThere is no return arrow. Give \\(P\\)-currents a small multiplier \\(\\delta_P^2\\), keeping equal multipliers on each Bianchi pair. The slice energy \\(\\mathcal E_T\\) is equivalent to\n\\[\n\\sum_i(Y_i^2+Z_i^2+z_i^2+N_i^2)+D_b^2+\\delta_P^2P^2.\n\\]\nGiven \\(\\rho_*&gt;0\\), choose \\(\\delta_P\\) so the limiting form costs at most \\(\\rho_*\\mathcal E_T/2\\), then a small threshold \\(h_*&gt;0\\) so the \\(H_s\\)-terms have the same bound when \\(H_s\\le h_*\\). Elsewhere the rate is bounded by a fixed \\(C_*\\), chosen before \\(\\nu\\).</p><p>Differences are supported where \\(v\\ge(1-\\nu)T\\). Choose \\(R,t_0\\) so envelope tails, \\(q\\), and \\(\\sigma\\) are small outside the indicated compact ranges. On the supported set with \\(t&gt;t_0\\), \\(h_1\\) is small for large \\(T\\); failure of \\(H_s\\le h_*\\) can occur only where \\(u\\ge-R\\). Such times satisfy\n\\[\n2t=u+v\\ge(1-\\nu)T-R,\\qquad t\\le T/2+O(1).\n\\]\nThe total bad-time length is at most\n\\[\nt_0+\\nu T/2+O(1).\n\\]\nThus the integrated homogeneous rate is an arbitrarily small multiple of \\(T\\), choosing \\(\\rho_*\\), then \\(\\nu\\).</p><p>Both optical directions have nonnegative flux on the tilted outgoing sides; the complete sphere gives no angular boundary. Defects add at most\n\\[\nC\\operatorname{poly}(T)F_T\\sqrt{\\mathcal E_T}\n\\]\nto the squared-energy inequality, and the initial discrepancy has the same finite-integration bound. The square-root inequality and Gronwall yield\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\mathcal E_T(t)}\n\\le C\\operatorname{poly}(T)A_T^2e^{\\eta T}\n\\exp\\{C\\rho_*T+C_*\\nu T/2+O(1)\\}\n\\le A_T^2e^{\\eta_2T}.\n\\]\nChoose all losses strictly below \\(\\eta_2\\) to absorb fixed and polynomial factors. This proves both conclusions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 6.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the exact whole-bridge data \\(d_T\\), exact exterior, and exact deep double-null experiment of Propositions 6–7, restricted inside their construction margins.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large finite \\(T\\), the exterior and deep experiments lie in a smooth globally hyperbolic vacuum development of the entire datum \\(d_T\\), which embeds isometrically in its full MGHD \\(\\mathcal M_{d_T}\\).</p><p>The whole initial bridge and exterior correction tail are retained. The common earlier branch tubes and compact observation region have interior margins in the attached region.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the qualitative lower/exterior joining construction [22, Section 7.1, Remark 7.2], based on [23, Lemma 8.1, Propositions 8.2, 9.10]. Its hypotheses are full-bridge \\(p_{10}\\) closeness, protected agreement on compact causal shadows, actual corrected partial-slice data, low exterior closeness on enlarged bands, and strict outflow and overlap margins.</p><p>The finite lower block applies because \\(p_{10}(d_T-d)\\to0\\). It contains every spatial radius of the new bridge, a past collar, both bent exterior initial slices, and fixed forward bands. The protected ball contains a fixed-origin ball of radius\n\\[\n(1-2\\beta-s_*)t_T-O(1)\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nHence all fixed compact causal shadows of the early joins are exactly unchanged, and domain of dependence identifies them with the background. At large radii the selected-end slice carries the actual new datum, including its tail.</p><p>Choose a fixed exterior joining time \\(t_a\\) inside the enlarged lower block. Attach the actual selected-end exterior using its shrinking outer cutoff. The identifying wave map on an enlarged fixed band has the prescribed slice map and matching normals. Fixed-time stability gives a diffeomorphism on the smaller band uniformly to the outer cutoff. Induced metric and second fundamental form agree; wave gauge determines the remaining normal jets, so reduced uniqueness makes the map an isometry. The other end uses the unchanged exterior. Identify overlaps without retaining duplicate points.</p><p>The lower block is not spatially truncated. In the selected-end overlap the retained region satisfies\n\\[\nt_e&lt;t_a\\quad\\text{or}\\quad r_{\\mathrm{co}}&lt;R_{\\mathrm{out}}(t_e).\n\\]\nBoth defining quantities increase along future causal curves at their active boundaries, since\n\\[\n\\frac d{dt_e}(r_{\\mathrm{co}}-R_{\\mathrm{out}})\n\\ge V_{\\mathrm{out}}-\\sup|dr_{\\mathrm{co}}/dt_e|&gt;0.\n\\]\nTheir strict sublevels and union are preserved to the past. A past curve in the added piece reaches \\(t_a\\) before leaving the cutoff, after which all radii are present. The omitted cutoff corner is not a missing past endpoint.</p><p>The required interval of \\(\\mathcal S\\), with margin, lies in the joined region. At the late selected end \\(t_e\\le t_T+O(1)\\) and co-radius is bounded; other parts are unchanged. Low closeness preserves spacelikeness. Use the actual exterior induced data. The prescribed eikonal roots and shift-free angles are noncharacteristic at fixed depth, and local vacuum uniqueness identifies their collar with the exact deep solution. The template is not used as matching data.</p><p>Above the join, use the new exact double-null coordinates. The causal inequality implies \\(du,dv\\ge0\\), while\n\\[\ng_T^{-1}(dt,dt)=-1/(2a_T)&lt;0.\n\\]\nFor nonzero future causal vectors,\n\\[\n\\frac d{dt}(u+c_0t/T)\\ge c_0/T,\\qquad\n\\frac d{dt}(v+c_0t/T)\\ge c_0/T.\n\\]\nThus all strict side and terminal sublevels are preserved to the past. A past-inextendible causal curve from the deep interior reaches \\(t=0\\) strictly within the attached interval. At fixed \\(T\\), a compact enlargement has smooth bounds on \\(a_T,a_T^{-1},b_T,\\gamma_T^{\\pm1}\\). In \\(t\\)-parameter, \\(0\\le du/dt,dv/dt\\le2\\), and the cone inequality bounds angular speed. Finite interior endpoints continue ordinarily.</p><p>Omit unattached future faces and edges. Their future-exit signs exclude past endpoints there. Finite unions and intersections of the past-preserved strict sublevels handle cut intersections. Matching collars give product neighborhoods; the joining construction is Hausdorff and retains one copy of each point.</p><p>Every past-inextendible causal curve from an added piece reaches the complete bridge. The lower Cauchy construction and bounded speeds exclude spatial escape in bounded time. The past collar gives the corresponding future crossing for curves below the bridge. The lower-block Cauchy property and one-way signs give at most one crossing. Therefore \\(\\Sigma\\) is Cauchy in the assembled vacuum development, and maximal-development uniqueness embeds it in \\(\\mathcal M_{d_T}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 6.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the common numerical double-null coordinates on the fixed enlarged observation box \\(Q_T\\), with background \\(g\\) and attached exact experiment \\(g_T\\). All feet lie in the launch-bump plateau.</p><p>Adopt\n\\[\nR(X,Y)=[\\nabla_X,\\nabla_Y]-\\nabla_{[X,Y]},\n\\qquad\nD(g)_{AB}=g(R(\\partial_v,\\partial_A)\\partial_v,\\partial_B).\n\\]\nUse the ordinary angular component norm.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every point of \\(Q_T\\) satisfying \\(|\\cos(\\lambda v)|\\ge1/2\\), and all sufficiently large \\(T\\),\n\\[\n|D(g_T)-D(g)|&gt;2A_T.\n\\]\nNo bound on \\(D(g)\\) or on background parallel-frame curvature is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>In the shift-free direction,\n\\[\n\\nabla_{L_1}L_1=\\ell_1L_1,\\qquad\n\\nabla_{\\partial_A}L_1=\\chi_{1,A}{}^B\\partial_B+\\xi_{1,A}L_1.\n\\]\nSince \\([\\partial_v,\\partial_A]=0\\), the curvature commutator and \\(\\partial_v\\gamma=2\\chi_1\\) give\n\\[\nD(g)=\\partial_v\\chi_1-\\chi_1\\gamma^{-1}\\chi_1-\\ell_1\\chi_1\n\\]\n\\[\n=\\tfrac12\\partial_v^2\\gamma\n-\\tfrac14(\\partial_v\\gamma)\\gamma^{-1}(\\partial_v\\gamma)\n-\\tfrac12(\\partial_v\\log a)(\\partial_v\\gamma).\n\\]\nThere is no inverse radial lapse.</p><p>For the template, \\(\\bar\\chi_1=\\chi_1+\\frac12\\partial_vk_\\gamma\\) and \\(\\bar\\ell_1=\\ell_1+\\partial_vk_H\\). The linear variation is\n\\[\n\\delta D=\\tfrac12\\partial_v^2k_\\gamma\n-\\tfrac14\\bigl((\\partial_vk_\\gamma)\\gamma^{-1}\\partial_v\\gamma\n+(\\partial_v\\gamma)\\gamma^{-1}\\partial_vk_\\gamma\\bigr)\n\\]\n\\[\n+\\tfrac14(\\partial_v\\gamma)\\gamma^{-1}k_\\gamma\\gamma^{-1}(\\partial_v\\gamma)\n-\\tfrac12\\ell_1\\partial_vk_\\gamma\n-\\tfrac12(\\partial_vk_H)\\partial_v\\gamma.\n\\]\nThe angular Lie term in \\(k_\\gamma\\) contains only \\(Z\\) and angular derivatives of \\(Z\\). Lemma 6.3 therefore gives\n\\[\n\\partial_v^2(\\mathcal L_Zg)_{AB}=O(A_T\\lambda e^{o(T)}),\n\\qquad\n\\partial_vk_H=O(A_T\\lambda e^{o(T)}).\n\\]\nAll other linear terms have this size. Twice differentiating the leading real oscillation gives\n\\[\nD(\\bar g)-D(g)\n=-\\tfrac12A_T\\lambda^2\\cos(\\lambda v)(p_0)_{AB}\n+O(A_T\\lambda e^{o(T)})\n+O(A_T^2\\lambda^Pe^{o(T)}).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\zeta\\) so the quadratic term is \\(o(A_T)\\). Proposition 7 gives \\(o(A_T)\\) exact-minus-template error in this relative-field expression, preserved under subexponential coefficient multiplication.</p><p>On the cosine-good set, the leading norm is at least\n\\[\n\\tfrac14A_T\\lambda^2e^{-o(T)}.\n\\]\nIts ratios to the lower-order linear error and to \\(A_T\\) tend to infinity. This proves the strict bound without estimating the background block itself.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 6.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(O\\in\\mathcal B\\) with \\(\\overline O\\subset N\\), and a closed concentric ball \\(O_0\\Subset O\\) such that\n\\[\n\\mu_d(O_0)&gt;.95\\,\\mu_d(O).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a closed interval \\(J_0\\Subset J\\) of positive length.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_v\\) be the background hitting map from \\(O_0\\) to the late \\(v\\)-section, recording\n\\[\nz=(u,\\theta,\\pi_u,P_1,P_2).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use these same numerical terminal states on the future unit shell of \\(g_T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nr_T=e^{-\\kappa T},\\qquad\nF=(\\partial_u,r_T^{-1}\\partial_v,\\partial_{\\theta^1},\\partial_{\\theta^2}).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The terminal states have injective backward seed maps\n\\[\n\\Psi_{T,v}:O_0\\to O,\n\\qquad\n\\sup_{v\\in T+J_0}\\|\\Psi_{T,v}-\\mathrm{incl}\\|_{C^1}\\to0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\Psi_{T,v}^*\\mu_{d_T}=\\mu_d.\n\\]\nThus \\(H_v(O_0)\\) has canonical volume \\(\\mu_d(O_0)\\).</p><p>On the larger observation box, the Gram matrices of \\(g\\) and \\(g_T\\) in \\(F\\), and their inverses, are uniformly bounded and nondegenerate. Their ordinary coordinate jets through order thirty are \\(e^{o(T)}\\). Selected unit tangents are bounded in \\(F\\); momenta lie in a fixed bounded box; and\n\\[\ncr_T\\,du\\,dv\\,d^2\\theta\n\\le d\\operatorname{vol}_{g},\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_{g_T}\n\\le Cr_T\\,du\\,dv\\,d^2\\theta.\n\\]</p><p>For each fixed \\(T\\) and each \\(\\eta_{\\mathrm{map}}&gt;0\\), a relative weighted-smooth neighborhood of \\(d_T\\) preserves these observations, with signal bound \\(A_T\\) instead of \\(2A_T\\). The common persisted coordinates need not be optical. Its backward maps \\(\\widehat\\Psi_{T,v}\\) remain injective, measure-preserving, and map into \\(O\\), with\n\\[\n\\sup_v\\|\\widehat\\Psi_{T,v}-\\Psi_{T,v}\\|_{C^1}&lt;\\eta_{\\mathrm{map}}.\n\\]\nThe neighborhood may depend on \\(T,\\eta_{\\mathrm{map}}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The unit mass shell is\n\\[\np=-(\\pi_u+b^AP_A)&gt;0,\\qquad\n2p\\pi_v+a(1+\\gamma^{AB}P_AP_B)=0.\n\\]\nThus the reduced Hamiltonian is\n\\[\nH=-\\pi_v=\\frac{a(1+\\gamma^{AB}P_AP_B)}{2p}.\n\\]\nThe background tube keeps \\(p\\) in a compact positive interval and momenta bounded. Since \\(b_T-b\\to0\\), the same holds for \\(p_T\\). The root with \\(\\dot v=p_T/a_T&gt;0\\) is future directed.</p><p>The background Hamiltonian field and its first two state derivatives are bounded on the late tube by \\(ae^{o(v)}\\), integrable in \\(v\\). The new field and these derivatives differ by an absolutely exponentially small quantity: every derivative of \\(a_T=qA_T^{\\mathrm{rel}}\\) retains \\(q\\), denominators are powers of \\(p_T\\) separated from zero, and fixed relative-field differences are exponentially small. Here \\(A_T^{\\mathrm{rel}}\\) denotes the experiment’s relative lapse, not the packet amplitude.</p><p>The terminal difference is zero. Backward Gronwall over an \\(O(T)\\) late interval gives state convergence. First variations satisfy \\(J'=D_zVJ\\); coefficient differences are the derivative-field discrepancy plus \\(D_z^2V\\) times the state discrepancy. A second Gronwall gives \\(C^1\\) convergence. The background forward hitting derivatives are uniformly bounded by\n\\[\n\\|D_wH_v\\|\n\\le\\|D_wH_{v_0}\\|\n\\exp\\int_{v_0}^v\\|D_zV(s)\\|\\,ds\\le C.\n\\]\nStrict margins prevent tube exit. The remaining earlier tubes are compact and transverse, and protected agreement makes them exactly background for large \\(T\\). Their finite-flow dependence completes the seed convergence and gives image in \\(O\\).</p><p>On \\(T^*\\mathcal M\\),\n\\[\n\\omega=d\\pi_u\\wedge du+d\\pi_v\\wedge dv+dP_A\\wedge d\\theta^A.\n\\]\nIts unit-shell restriction has geodesic-flow kernel. On a \\(v\\)-section it is\n\\[\n\\omega_v=d\\pi_u\\wedge du+dP_A\\wedge d\\theta^A,\n\\]\nindependently of the root \\(\\pi_v\\). Its canonical volume is\n\\[\ndu\\,d^2\\theta\\,d\\pi_u\\,d^2P.\n\\]\nTransverse hitting preserves this volume, proving the exact measure identity. Flow uniqueness gives injectivity; alternatively, \\(C^1\\) closeness to inclusion gives \\(\\mathrm{Id}+f\\), \\(\\|Df\\|&lt;1/2\\), injective on the convex ball. No injectivity of position projection is assumed.</p><p>At observation,\n\\[\n\\dot u=\\frac{1+\\gamma^{AB}P_AP_B}{2p},\n\\qquad\nr_T\\dot v=\\frac{r_Tp}{a},\n\\qquad\n\\dot\\theta=b\\dot u+\\gamma^{-1}P.\n\\]\nSince \\(u,v-T\\) are bounded, \\(a/r_T\\) is bounded above and below. The tangent bounds follow. The Gram matrix is\n\\[\nG_F=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\gamma(b,b)&amp;-a/r_T&amp;-b^C\\gamma_{CB}\\\\\n-a/r_T&amp;0&amp;0\\\\\n-\\gamma_{AC}b^C&amp;0&amp;\\gamma_{AB}\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\det G_F=-(a/r_T)^2\\det\\gamma.\n\\]\nLow field bounds give bounded entries and inverse. Ordinary derivatives of \\(a/r_T\\) preserve its relative factor, and other jets are subexponential. Finally,\n\\[\n\\sqrt{|\\det g|}=a\\sqrt{\\det\\gamma}\n\\]\ngives the volume comparison.</p><p>For fixed \\(T\\), the observation region and all backward tubes have compact interior margins by Proposition 8. Compact Cauchy stability gives corresponding finite-coordinate witnesses for nearby data. Require sufficiently accurate finite \\(C^k\\) closeness, including in the rescaled frame, to preserve the Gram, inverse, thirty-jet, density, and curvature margins:\n\\[\n|D(\\widehat g_T)-D(g)|&gt;A_T\n\\quad\\text{when }|\\cos(\\lambda v)|\\ge1/2.\n\\]</p><p>For a persisted metric, solve\n\\[\n\\tfrac12\\widehat g_T^{-1}(\\pi,\\pi)=-\\tfrac12\n\\]\nfor \\(\\pi_v\\) at fixed remaining terminal coordinates. The derivative is \\(U^v&gt;0\\), separated from zero on the fixed compact set, so the simple future root persists. Smooth backward-flow dependence gives the prescribed map tolerance while preserving injectivity and image margin. The section symplectic form remains \\(\\omega_v\\) even without optical coordinates. All assertions concern this fixed finite experiment.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 7.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For each experiment set\n\\[\nr=e^{-\\kappa T},\\qquad l_T=e^{-\\kappa T/16},\\qquad A_T=e^{-\\kappa T/4},\n\\]\nand use the commuting frame\n\\[\nF_0=\\partial_u,\\quad F_1=r^{-1}\\partial_v,\\quad\nF_2=\\partial_{\\theta^1},\\quad F_3=\\partial_{\\theta^2}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_{T,v}=H_v(O_0)\\) be the background terminal states from Proposition 10, and define\n\\[\nP_T=\\{(v,u,\\theta,\\pi_u,P):v\\in T+J_0,\\ (u,\\theta,\\pi_u,P)\\in P_{T,v}\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nd\\nu_T=dv\\,du\\,d^2\\theta\\,d\\pi_u\\,d^2P.\n\\]\nThe momentum labels range in a fixed bounded box \\(Q\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\), and\n\\[\n\\nu_T(P_T)=|J_0|\\mu_d(O_0).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g\\) be either the background or a persisted experiment satisfying Proposition 10. On a larger observation box, its Gram matrix \\(G_{ij}=g(F_i,F_j)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|G\\|+\\|G^{-1}\\|\\le C,\\qquad\n\\sum_{|\\beta|\\le30}(\\|\\partial^\\beta G\\|+\\|\\partial^\\beta G^{-1}\\|)\n\\le Q_T,\\qquad Q_T=e^{o(T)}.\n\\]\nSelected future unit tangents have bounded \\(F\\)-components.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix one extension and at most \\(B\\) charts with the whole-chart bounds of the test definition. Let \\(G_T\\subset P_T\\) be the measurable parameters whose base point and tangent satisfy the margin and velocity conditions in some chart of this collection.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a measurable exceptional set \\(N_T\\subset G_T\\) such that\n\\[\n\\nu_T(N_T)\\le Ce^{-\\kappa T/16},\n\\]\nand on \\(G_T\\setminus N_T\\),\n\\[\n|D(g)|\\le Ce^{-13\\kappa T/32}+Q_Te^{-\\kappa T}=o(A_T).\n\\]\nConstants depend only on \\(B\\) and the uniform observation bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>The determinant relation gives\n\\[\nd\\operatorname{vol}_g=r\\sqrt{|\\det G|}\\,du\\,dv\\,d^2\\theta,\n\\qquad\ncr\\le\\sqrt{|\\det g_{\\mathrm{coord}}|}\\le Cr.\n\\]\nAt each good parameter select the least admissible chart index. Fix Borel representatives of the weak Christoffel arrays. Lemma 11.1 gives\n\\[\n\\|M(0)\\|+\\|M(0)^{-1}\\|\\le C_0.\n\\]\nChoose \\(K&gt;2C_0+1\\).</p><p>For each \\(X\\in\\mathcal X\\) and sampled size \\(l=jl_T\\), \\(1\\le j\\le17\\), use the selected base chart on the four concatenated edges. Stop first when either \\(\\|M\\|=K\\) or the chart displacement from the base reaches \\(1/(2B)\\). The base margin \\(1/B\\) keeps the latter stopped path in the chart. Pre-stop conditions are Borel, expressed through rational-time suprema on the smooth interior domain.</p><p>Write \\(x=(v,u,\\theta)\\), \\(m=(\\pi_u,P)\\). At fixed edge parameter the position is translated by\n\\[\nsl\\partial_v,\\quad\nl\\partial_v+slrX,\\quad\n(1-s)l\\partial_v+lrX,\\quad\n(1-s)lrX.\n\\]\nThe translation is independent of \\(m\\).</p><p>Restrict first to parameters with selected chart \\(c\\) before the global stop, then enlarge the nonnegative integral to all \\(m\\in Q\\) and translated observation positions lying in that chart. The translated position, isometric embedding, and chart form an injective smooth coordinate map. Its volume change is controlled without an along-edge derivative estimate:\n\\[\ndx\\le Cr^{-1}d\\operatorname{vol}_g\n=Cr^{-1}\\sqrt{|\\det\\widetilde g_c|}\\,dy_c\n\\le C_Br^{-1}dy_c.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\int_{\\substack{G_T:\\ c\\text{ selected}\\\\\\text{before stop}}}\n|\\widetilde\\Gamma_c(y_c(x+c_e(s,l)))|^2\\,d\\nu_T\n\\le \\frac{C_B|Q|}{r}\\int|\\widetilde\\Gamma_c(y)|^2\\,dy.\n\\]\nMomentum-dependent selection and stopping only restrict the integral. No unproved bound on \\(M\\) is used in the volume change.</p><p>The weak array equals the smooth interior array almost everywhere. At fixed \\(s\\), smooth coordinate changes pull position null sets back to null sets; Fubini in \\(s,x,m\\) removes a parameter-null set for each sampled loop. Thus stopped integrals use the smooth connection equation without claiming traces on prescribed loops.</p><p>For\n\\[\nI_e(x,m)=\\int_0^1\\mathbf1_{\\{\\text{before stop}\\}}\n|\\widetilde\\Gamma_c(y_e(s))|\\,ds,\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz, chart summation, and the \\(L^2\\) bounds give\n\\[\n\\int_{G_T}I_e^2\\,d\\nu_T\\le C/r.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nR_T=r^{-1/2}e^{\\kappa T/32}=r^{-17/32}.\n\\]\nMarkov gives\n\\[\n\\nu_T\\{I_e&gt;R_T\\}\\le(C/r)R_T^{-2}=Ce^{-\\kappa T/16}.\n\\]\nUnion over four edges, seventeen sizes, and three \\(X\\)’s, together with the discarded null sets, defines \\(N_T\\). Only finitely many sizes are used.</p><p>For a remaining parameter, write the edge’s \\(F\\)-velocity \\(f=lrV\\). Lemma 11.2 bounds its internal connection matrix by \\(lQ_T\\). The exact transformation law is\n\\[\nM'=MA-\\widetilde\\Gamma_c(Mf)M.\n\\]\nBefore the stop,\n\\[\n|M'|\\le KlQ_T+CK^2lr|\\widetilde\\Gamma_c|,\n\\qquad\n|y'|\\le CKlr.\n\\]\nSumming all stopped edges gives\n\\[\n\\sup\\|M-M(0)\\|\\le4KlQ_T+CK^2lrR_T=o(1),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sup|y-y(0)|\\le CKlr=o(1).\n\\]\nIndeed \\(l\\le17e^{-\\kappa T/16}\\) and \\(lrR_T\\le17e^{-17\\kappa T/32}\\). These strictly improve both thresholds, so continuity rules out a first stop on any edge.</p><p>Chart transport has total integrated coefficient at most\n\\[\nClr\\sum_eI_e\\le Clr^{1/2}e^{\\kappa T/32}.\n\\]\nSince the rectangle closes, the final frame matrix equals its base matrix. Conjugating chart transport by bounded \\(M(0),M(0)^{-1}\\) gives\n\\[\n\\|H_{x,X}(jl_T)-\\mathrm{Id}\\|\n\\le Cr^{1/2}l_Te^{\\kappa T/32},\n\\qquad 1\\le j\\le17.\n\\]\nAt \\(j=0\\) it is zero.</p><p>Write\n\\[\nH_{x,X}(l)-\\mathrm{Id}\n=\\sum_{k=0}^{17}C_kl^k+\\mathcal R_{18}(l).\n\\]\nLemma 11.2 bounds the remainder at sampled nodes by \\(CQ_Tl_T^{18}\\). Inverting the fixed Vandermonde matrix \\((j^k)_{0\\le j,k\\le17}\\) yields\n\\[\n\\|C_2\\|\n\\le Cl_T^{-2}\\max_j\\|H_{x,X}(jl_T)-\\mathrm{Id}\\|\n+Q_Tl_T^{16}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\le Cr^{1/2}l_T^{-1}e^{\\kappa T/32}+Q_Tl_T^{16}\n=Ce^{-13\\kappa T/32}+Q_Te^{-\\kappa T}.\n\\]\nBoth rates improve on \\(A_T=e^{-\\kappa T/4}\\).</p><p>The coefficient identity and bounded \\(G\\) control \\(D(X,X)\\) for the three \\(X\\)’s. Curvature symmetry gives \\(D_{12}=D_{21}\\), and\n\\[\n2D_{12}\n=D(\\partial_{\\theta^1}+\\partial_{\\theta^2},\n\\partial_{\\theta^1}+\\partial_{\\theta^2})-D_{11}-D_{22}.\n\\]\nThis controls the entire block as claimed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the observation setting of Proposition 11. At a good base point, suppose the extension-chart metric and inverse are bounded and the selected unit tangent \\(U\\) has bounded components both in the chart and in \\(F\\). Let \\(M\\) have columns \\(F_i\\) in chart coordinates.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both \\(M\\) and \\(M^{-1}\\) are bounded in terms of these bounds and the uniform Gram bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a Lorentzian Gram matrix \\(G\\) and unit timelike column \\(U\\), put\n\\[\nK(G,U)=G+2(GU)(GU)^t.\n\\]\nWriting a vector as \\(aU+Z\\), \\(g(U,Z)=0\\), gives the positive form \\(a^2+g(Z,Z)\\). The determinant lemma gives\n\\[\n\\det K(G,U)=\\det G(1+2U^tGU)=-\\det G.\n\\]\nBounds on \\(G,G^{-1},U\\) give an upper eigenvalue bound and a positive determinant lower bound, hence a positive smallest-eigenvalue bound.</p><p>If \\(g_c\\) is the chart metric and \\(U_c=MU_F\\), then\n\\[\nK(G,U_F)=M^tK(g_c,U_c)M.\n\\]\nThe two positive-definite comparisons bound \\(M\\) and its inverse. Bounded Lorentzian Gram matrices alone are not used to bound a Lorentz boost.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 11 and set\n\\[\n\\mathcal X=\\{\\partial_{\\theta^1},\\partial_{\\theta^2},\n\\partial_{\\theta^1}+\\partial_{\\theta^2}\\}.\n\\]\nFor a base position \\(x\\), \\(X\\in\\mathcal X\\), and \\(0\\le l\\le17l_T\\), take the coordinate rectangle with consecutive displacements\n\\[\nl\\partial_v,\\quad lrX,\\quad-l\\partial_v,\\quad-lrX.\n\\]\nLet \\(H_{x,X}(l)\\) be its holonomy in the based frame \\(F(x)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly in \\(x,X\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_{0\\le l\\le17l_T}\\|\\partial_l^{18}H_{x,X}(l)\\|\\le Q_T.\n\\]\nThe quadratic Taylor coefficient \\(C_2(x,X)\\) of \\(H_{x,X}(l)-\\mathrm{Id}\\) is the \\(F(x)\\)-matrix of \\(\\pm R(\\partial_v,rX)\\). Consequently,\n\\[\ng(C_2(x,X)F_1,X)=\\pm D(g)(X,X).\n\\]\nThe curvature and loop-orientation conventions fix the signs.</p>", "proof": "<p>The rectangles remain in the enlarged observation box since ordinary displacement is \\(O(l)\\). Their edge velocities in \\(F\\) have scale \\(lr\\).</p><p>Because \\(F\\) commutes, its connection coefficients are\n\\[\nC^k{}_{ij}\n=\\tfrac12G^{kh}(F_iG_{jh}+F_jG_{ih}-F_hG_{ij}).\n\\]\nFor a constant column \\(V\\) and \\(W=rV^iF_i\\), the connection matrix is\n\\[\nA(W)^k{}_j\n=\\tfrac12G^{kh}V^i\\bigl((rF_i)G_{jh}+(rF_j)G_{ih}-(rF_h)G_{ij}\\bigr).\n\\]\nEach \\(rF_i\\) is one of \\(r\\partial_u,\\partial_v,r\\partial_{\\theta^A}\\), with bounded constant coefficients. Thus contraction with \\(\\partial_v\\) or \\(rX\\), and eighteen ordinary derivatives, costs only \\(Q_T\\). Uncontracted \\(F\\)-connection coefficients need not satisfy this bound.</p><p>An edge has position \\(x+c_e(s,l)\\), linear in \\(l\\), independent of momentum, and transport coefficient \\(lA(\\pm W)(x+c_e(s,l))\\). Its integral norm is \\(lQ_T=o(1)\\). Differentiating in \\(l\\) eighteen times uses only bounded ordinary coordinate directions. For \\(P'=-A_eP\\),\n\\[\n(\\partial_l^kP)'\n=-A_e\\partial_l^kP\n-\\sum_{j=1}^k\\binom kj(\\partial_l^jA_e)(\\partial_l^{k-j}P).\n\\]\nGronwall uses the small undifferentiated integral; induction gives subexponential differentiated transports. Multiplying four edge transports proves the jet bound.</p><p>To identify the coefficient, pull back the smooth connection by\n\\[\n(s,t)\\mapsto x+s\\partial_v+trX.\n\\]\nChoose a smooth auxiliary frame agreeing with \\(F\\) at the origin whose connection form vanishes there. On the boundary of \\([0,l]^2\\), its integral norm is \\(O(l^2)\\). Ordered transport equals identity minus the boundary integral up to \\(O(l^4)\\). Stokes identifies the quadratic coefficient with the curvature on \\((\\partial_v,rX)\\), with orientation sign. This frame is used only to identify the coefficient.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\ng(R(\\partial_v,rX)(r^{-1}\\partial_v),X)\n=rr^{-1}R(\\partial_v,X,\\partial_v,X),\n\\]\nso there is no remaining factor of \\(r\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Theorem 7.4, used as the final intermediate category statement", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the single neighborhood \\(\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\) of Proposition 1 and the existential tests \\(E(O,B)\\) of Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(O\\in\\mathcal B\\) and integer \\(B\\ge1\\), the set \\(E(O,B)\\) is relatively nowhere dense in \\(\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose a nonempty relative open set \\(V\\subset\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\) is contained in \\(\\overline{E(O,B)}\\). Then \\(E(O,B)\\) is dense in \\(V\\); choose\n\\[\nd\\in E(O,B)\\cap V.\n\\]\nSince the seminorms increase, there are finite \\(m\\ge10\\) and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\{d'\\in\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon:p_m(d'-d)&lt;\\eta\\}\\subset V.\n\\]</p><p>Fix this datum’s branch tube and extension witness. Propositions 6–8 produce exact whole-bridge packet data \\(d_T\\) along the tube’s unbounded admissible sequence, with \\(p_m(d_T-d)\\to0\\). Thus \\(d_T\\in V\\) for all large \\(T\\).</p><p>By density and the finite persistence of Proposition 10, choose\n\\[\n\\widehat d_T\\in E(O,B)\\cap V\n\\]\nas close to \\(d_T\\) as required by all finite observation conditions, also requiring \\(p_0(\\widehat d_T-d_T)\\to0\\). On the common tube,\n\\[\n|D(\\widehat g_T)-D(g_d)|&gt;A_T\n\\quad\\text{if }|\\cos(\\lambda v)|\\ge1/2.\n\\]\nBoth metrics retain the uniform hypotheses of Proposition 11. Their constants are independent of \\(T\\), by the finite closeness imposed. Smooth dependence of the initial section density gives\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mu_{\\widehat d_T}(O)}{\\mu_d(O)}\\to1.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(S,\\widehat S_T\\subset O\\) be their respective test-good seed families, each with more than \\(99\\) percent of its own measure of \\(O\\). Use the common numerical terminal parameters from the background hits of \\(O_0\\).</p><p>At a fixed \\(v\\), background seeds outside \\(S\\) lose at most \\(.01\\mu_d(O)\\). The matched backward map for \\(\\widehat g_T\\) is injective into \\(O\\) and preserves section volume, so seeds outside \\(\\widehat S_T\\) lose at most \\(.01\\mu_{\\widehat d_T}(O)\\). For large \\(T\\), combined loss is less than \\(.04\\mu_d(O)\\). Since \\(\\mu_d(O_0)&gt;.95\\mu_d(O)\\), the parameters good for both, denoted \\(G_T^\\cap\\), satisfy\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}_6(G_T^\\cap\\cap\\{v=\\text{constant}\\})\n&gt;.91\\,\\mu_d(O).\n\\]\nDependence on \\(v\\) is measurable. The witnesses apply because the test bounds hold throughout each full future lifetime, and all matched observations are interior points.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nI_T=\\{v\\in T+J_0:|\\cos(\\lambda v)|\\ge1/2\\}.\n\\]\nThis occupies \\(2/3\\) of each full period. Only endpoint periods are incomplete, so\n\\[\n|I_T|=\\tfrac23|J_0|+O(\\lambda^{-1})&gt;\\tfrac12|J_0|\n\\]\nfor large \\(T\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\nu_T(G_T^\\cap\\cap\\{v\\in I_T\\})\n&gt;.455\\,\\mu_d(O)|J_0|.\n\\]</p><p>Apply Proposition 11 separately to \\(g_d\\) and \\(\\widehat g_T\\). The union of their additional exceptional sets has measure tending to zero and cannot cover this positive-measure set. At a remaining parameter,\n\\[\n|D(g_d)|=o(A_T),\\qquad |D(\\widehat g_T)|=o(A_T),\n\\]\nuniformly, whereas their difference exceeds \\(A_T\\). This is a contradiction.</p><p>Thus \\(\\overline{E(O,B)}\\) has empty relative interior. No closedness of the test itself is used.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 2, "cite"], [1, 8, "cite"], [1, 22, "cite"], [2, 8, "cite"], [3, 8, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [4, 8, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 4, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 4, "cite"], [8, 0, "cite"], [8, 22, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [9, 16, "cite"], [9, 22, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [12, 17, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [13, 17, "cite"], [13, 22, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [16, 22, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 22, "cite"], [19, 22, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [22, 0, "cite"]]});
